VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aRICHARDSON, ANDREA, L., M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by RICHARDSON,ANDREA L.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, 11 G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aFocal atypical hyperplasia with associated calcifications, (best seen on\u000adeeper levels of block 1).\u000aThe atypical proliferation has features of lobular neoplasia but with\u000amicroacini suggestive of atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aAtrophic breast tissue with calcifications.\u000aNOTE: The atypical hyperplasia is best evaluated on surgical excision.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 66 yo, focal ca++s LUOQ, slightly increased over time.\u000aOperation: 11g stereotactic.\u000aOperative findings: Ca++s in specimens.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Probable sclerosing adenosis.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Left breast tissue in two orange cassettes. A - tissue with\u000aca++. B - remainder of tissue.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by O'CONNELL,FIONNUALA,MB,BCH,BAO\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "left breast", and consists of two (2) orange cassettes. One\u000acassette is labeled "A ca++" and contains multiple, soft, fragmented, yellow\u000ato white tissue cores (1.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The other cassette\u000ais labeled "B" and contains similar tissue fragments (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000aaggregate).\u000aMicro 1: "A ca++", multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 2: "B", multi frags, ESS.\u000adfi\u000aReports to: DENISON, CHRISTINE M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY(IES)\u000a[report_end]
