VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Elizabeth A. Steensma, M.D.; Rachel L Rucker-Schmidt, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, LATERAL, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, moderately differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade II/III), measuring REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aImmunoperoxidase stain for e-cadherin is positive in tumor cells, supporting\u000athe above diagnosis.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is present.\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, clinging type (intermediate nuclear grade), present\u000ain one (1) of six (6) blocks, associated with the invasive tumor (EIC\u000anegative).\u000aMedial, lateral, superior, inferior, anterior, and posterior resection margins\u000aare negative (>0.3 cm) for invasive and in situ tumor.\u000aIntraductal papilloma with atypia (1.2 cm), associated with core biopsy site.\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia.\u000aMultiple levels examined.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor - POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor - POSTIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) - NEGATIVE (0)\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor cells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but only a\u000aportion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10% of\u000atumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in >10%\u000aof tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST, MEDIAL, WIRE LOCALISED EXCISION FOR AN AREA OF ARCHITECTURAL\u000aDISTORTION:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid type (intermediate nuclear grade), present as\u000aone focus (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The DCIS is not associated with necrosis or\u000acalcifications.\u000aThe anterior, posterior, inferior, superior, lateral, and medial margins are\u000afree (>0.3 cm) of DCIS.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aDuctal epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aColumnar cell changes.\u000aIntraductal papilloma.\u000aStromal calcifications.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aMultiple levels examined.\u000aNOTE: This case was reviewed by Dr. James Connolly, breast pathology, who\u000aagrees with the above diagnosis.\u000aDr. Shoji was electronically notified on REMOVED_DATE  at noon, by Dr. Steensma.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Abnormal left mammogram/MRI\u000aOperation: needle loc breast biopsies\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast lateral specimen, short stitch superior long stitch lateral -\u000aFS mammo.\u000aB/2. Left medial breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral -\u000aFS mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000a1) First specimen image with intact hookwire and apparent oval mass in\u000aspecimen.\u000a2) Second specimen image with intact hookwire and apparent tissue density\u000awithin specimen. GGAMA discussed with Dr. Shoji @ 2:47 (ILLEGIBLE)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast lateral specimen short - stitch superior, long stitch\u000alateral", consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue,\u000aoriented by sutures designating superior (short suture) and lateral (long\u000asuture) margins. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange (inked by prosector). A\u000alocalizing wire enters the specimen in lateral aspect and traverses medially.\u000aSpecimen is received with an accompanying radiograph showing the intact\u000alocalizing wire in place within an ill-defined oval mass in the central portion\u000aof the specimen. Sectioning in this area demonstrates a well-circumscribed REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm friable, papillary-appearing tan/white mass, grossly REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe closest superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom the closest posterior margin, and remote (greater than REMOVED_DATE cm) from the\u000ainferior, medial and lateral margins. Remaining tissue is remarkable for focal\u000ahemorrhage along the localizing wire tract. There is scant fibrous tissue\u000a(specimen is at least 95% unremarkable adipose tissue), and no additional\u000alesions grossly. Mass and surrounding tissue are entirely submitted, to\u000ainclude closest margins.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire mass, sequentially from medial to lateral, 3 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue just medial to mass, representative medial margin, 3 frags\u000atotal, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Tissue just lateral to mass, representative lateral margin, 3 frags,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Closest inferior margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Left medial breast tissue short stitch superior, long stitch\u000alateral", consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of fibroadipose tissue,\u000aoriented by sutures designating superior (short suture) and lateral (long\u000asuture) margins. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange (inked by prosector). A\u000alocalizing wire enters the inferior aspect of the specimen and exits\u000asuperiorly. Specimen is received with an accompanying radiograph demonstrating\u000athe intact localizing wire in place within the specimen, marking an apparent\u000atissue density at the specimen's superior aspect. Please note: Specimen is\u000afocally disrupted superiorly; specimen is partially reconstructed prior to\u000ainking and true surgical margin in this area is difficult to assess.\u000aSectioning in this area demonstrates a palpably firm, ill-defined REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a1.7 cm tan/white lesion, which abuts the superior, posterior and medial\u000amargins, and approaches within REMOVED_DATE cm of the closest anterior margin. Lesion\u000ais at least REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest lateral and inferior margins. Remaining\u000aspecimen is remarkable for focal fat necrosis, but no additional masses,\u000alesions or other abnormalities. Tissue is predominantly tan/yellow adipose\u000atissue (at least 95%), admixed with occasional scant fibrous tissue. Entire\u000aspecimen is submitted, sequentially from superior to inferior, with\u000aperpendicular superior margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and perpendicular inferior margin in\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID. Lesion is predominantly in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen, sequentially from superior to inferior, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aDB/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
