VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aGLICKMAN, JONATHAN, NEIL, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by GLICKMAN,JONATHAN NEIL,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP:\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia (see NOTE).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (block #6).\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with benign epithelium.\u000aCore biopsy site changes.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aNOTE:\u000aCase reviewed by Dr. S. Lester who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 75-year-old female status post REMOVED_DATE  right breast stereotactic\u000acore biopsy of a 5 mm cluster of central calcifications revealing\u000aatypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aOperation: Right breast wire localization excision.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast calcifications/ADH.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. Right breast tissue, short stitch superior, long\u000astitch lateral, and whipstitch deep margin.\u000aRadiology Report:\u000aBiopsy clip within specimen.\u000a(no doctor initials on radiology report)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by WU,DAVID,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1, right breast tissue", and consists of a single fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which has been previously inked. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior = black; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral\u000a= yellow; medial = orange. There is a localizing wire protruding from the\u000aposterior lateral aspect of the specimen and the specimen is accompanied by a\u000aradiograph showing that the biopsy clip and localizing wire are within the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from lateral to medial revealing\u000adense fibrous breast parenchyma consisting of 80% fibrous tissue and 20%\u000aadipose tissue. A clip is identified within the specimen with adjacent\u000ahemorrhage and fibrosis consistent with core biopsy site changes. The entire\u000aspecimen is submitted for histological evaluation.\u000aMicro 1: lateral margin, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of specimen, submitted from lateral to medial (clip\u000awithin micro 5), multi frags, ESS.\u000ajs\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
