VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Andrew G. Evans, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT AXILLARY SENTINEL LYMPH NODE (INCLUDING REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aFour (4) sentinel lymph nodes negative for carcinoma (3 H&E levels examined).\u000aB. LEFT BREAST SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aCARCINOMA IN SITU with mixed ductal and lobular features, solid and\u000amicropapillary types (intermediate & high nuclear grade) with necrosis and\u000acalcifications is present in 10 of 28 slides. CIS involves nipple lactiferous\u000asinuses and is present in excision cavity wall spanning the width of the\u000aexcision cavity (5-7 cm)\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aNo residual invasive carcinoma seen.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion present.\u000aThe deep margin is negative for carcinoma.\u000aSkin negative for carcinoma.\u000aTwo axillary lymph nodes negative for carcinoma.\u000aOther findings:\u000aIntraductal papillomas with sclerosis.\u000aColumnar cell change with cysts and with extensive microcalcifications.\u000aAJCC Classification (6th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDb REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID(sn) including prior specimens\u000aC. RIGHT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL PROLIFERATION, with associated microcalcifications,\u000asee NOTE.\u000aExtensive calcifications in association with columnar cell change, with\u000aepithelial hyperplasia and cysts.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: A cluster of ducts spanning < REMOVED_DATE cm is present containing pleomorphic\u000ahigh nuclear grade cells (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID levels REMOVED_DATE ). The differential includes a small\u000afocus of DCIS, but given the size of the lesion the favored diagnosis is a\u000aseverely atypical ductal proliferation rather than DCIS. The atypical focus is\u000anot present on deeper levels used for special studies (ER, PR, Her2 & p63). The\u000aatypical focus is > REMOVED_DATE cm from all margins. Dr. Susan Lester and Dr. Andrea\u000aRichardson also reviewed REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and concur with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: CA of Lt breast.\u000aOperation: SNB, Lt ax.; Lt mastex; wire-directed excis.\u000aOperative Findings: Microcalcs of Rt breast\u000aClinical Diagnosis: CA of Lt breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left axillary sentinel lymph node (FS)\u000aB/2) Left breast, long suture lateral, short suture superior\u000aC/3) Right breast tissue\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#1. SENT. LYMPH NODE(S), LEFT AXILLA" (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aTwo (2) lymph nodes, negative for tumor\u000aOR Consultation by: Jeffrey Fred Krane, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aResident: Christian J. Davidson, M.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left axillary sentinel lymph node for frozen section".\u000aThe specimen consists of two lymph nodes (2.4 and REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest\u000adimension). Each is focally blue consistent with sentinel lymph node ink. The\u000afat is trimmed from each and they are bisected. The larger lymph node is\u000asubmitted for frozen section analysis as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID with one fragment in\u000aeach. The small lymph nodes were submitted for frozen section analysis as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000acontaining two fragments.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Nonfrozen remnants of specimen including a component of apparent\u000alymph node tissue, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Left breast, long suture lateral, short suture superior".\u000aThe specimen consists of a REMOVED_DATE gram simple mastectomy (18.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000acontaining a skin ellipse (13.3 cm x REMOVED_DATE cm), areola (4.0 cm in greatest\u000adimension) and everted nipple (1.1 cm). The skin ellipse contains a\u000awell-healed scar (3.0 cm medial to the areola running horizontally). The\u000aposterior aspect of the specimen contains a smooth fascial plane, which has\u000abeen inked black. The breast parenchyma contains an ill-defined firm mass (7.0\u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is in association with a hemorrhagic cyst/cavity (4.0 x\u000a2.8 x REMOVED_DATE cm). The mass is within REMOVED_DATE cm of the deep margin. The mass is\u000acentrally located in the inferior portion of the breast. The center of the\u000afirm mass is focal area of hemorrhage and fat necrosis consistent with biopsy\u000acavity. The remainder of the breast parenchyma is 90% adipose tissue and no\u000agrossly identifiable masses or lesions are present. The axillary tail (6.0 cm\u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm x REMOVED_DATE cm) contains one grossly identifiable lymph node candidate.\u000aThere are no radiology report or image to accompany the specimen. Gross\u000aphotographs were taken to document the extent of the mass and the hemorrhagic\u000acavity.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass to nearest deep surgical resection margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass immediately anterior to previous section, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass adjacent to hemorrhagic cavity, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Mass in vicinity of probable biopsy cavity, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Additional mass, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper outer quadrant, representative section , 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower outer quadrant, representative section , 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lower inner quadrant, representative section , 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Upper inner quadrant, representative section , 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Axillary lymph node bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Skin with nipple, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Additional sampling of excision cavity wall from medial to\u000alateral, multiple frags, RSS.\u000aPart C, labeled "#3. Right breast tissue". The specimen consists of a segment\u000aof fibroadipose tissue consistent with breast (4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is serially sectioned medial to lateral to reveal\u000aa firm ill-defined tan/white mass occupying greater than 90% of the specimen.\u000aAt the center of the mass are focal areas of hemorrhage and fat necrosis. The\u000aspecimen is accompanied by a radiology report within centricity that reads\u000a"Targeted microcalcifications and no wire. Dr. Christian called Dr. Alencar\u000aand states he has intact wire on the OR?. The specimen is entirely submitted\u000afor microscopic analysis.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections through the section of breast and associated mass,\u000a1 frag each, ESS.\u000aAEV/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aFriday REMOVED_MONTH 18, 2009 at 06:25:57PM\u000a[report_end]
