VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Consult material\u000aProcedure Date:\u000aOrdering Provider: ERIC P WINER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Martin C Chang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aCONSULT SLIDES FROM MASSACHUSETTS GENERAL HOSPITAL, BOSTON, MA:\u000aLEFT BREAST, DOUBLE WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND\u000aMRI-ENHANCEMENT ADJACENT TO SEROMA AND EXCISION OF MARGINS (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID;\u000a10/11/06):\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid and cribriform types (low to intermediate\u000anuclear grade) is present in 8 of 10 blocks in the main excision, by report\u000aover an area of 6 cm, adjacent to biopsy cavity. Calcifications are associated\u000awith DCIS. No necrosis is associated with DCIS.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is present.\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present.\u000aDCIS is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior (blue) resection margin in the main\u000aexcision, and < REMOVED_DATE cm from the margins in the separately submitted posterior,\u000asuperior, inferior, medial, and lateral shave margins.\u000aA papilloma is present.\u000aSpecial studies were performed at an outside institution with the following\u000aresults in DCIS (not reviewed):\u000aESTROGEN RECEPTOR POSITIVE\u000aPROGESTERONE RECEPTOR POSITIVE\u000aNOTE: The patient underwent a prior left breast subareolar excisional biopsy\u000awhich showed low grade DCIS, cribriform type and LCIS (ER and PR positive,\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu negative) (Faulkner Hospital, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; REMOVED_DATE ). The specimen was\u000acompletely examined microscopically.\u000aThe main excision in this case measured REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. No gross lesions\u000awere identified. Ten representative blocks of tissue were examined. The\u000aadditional margin excisions were small and were completely examined\u000amicroscopically. No stromal invasion or lymphovascular invasion are seen.\u000aBILATERAL MASTECTOMIES AND SENTINEL NODE BIOPSIES (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; REMOVED_DATE ):\u000aLEFT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY AND SENTINEL NODE BIOPSIES:\u000aRESIDUAL DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid, cribriform, and micropapillary types\u000a(low to intermediate nuclear grade) is present in 9 of 22 slides, adjacent to\u000athe biopsy cavity. Calcifications are associated with DCIS. No necrosis is\u000aassociated with DCIS.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is present.\u000aEXTENSIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present.\u000aThe deep resection margin is free of DCIS (> REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aSkin with scar.\u000aBy report, isolated tumor cells were present in one of two sentinel lymph\u000anodes. The cells were only seen on the immunohistochemical studies for\u000acytokeratin, and these slides are not received for review. The second node had\u000a"two small foci of epithelioid cytokeratin-positive cells". These were "not\u000afelt to represent unequivocal evidence of carcinoma."\u000aNOTE: Any clinical significance of isolated tumor cells in cases of DCIS is\u000aunclear. In one study (Lara, JF, et al, The relevance of occult axillary\u000amicrometastases in ductal carcinoma in situ. A clinicopathologic study with\u000along-term follow-up, Cancer 98:2105-2113, 2003), at 10 years of follow-up, all\u000apatients with DCIS and isolated tumor cells were free of recurrence.\u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition)\u000aTis REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (i+) (sn).\u000aRIGHT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY, AND SENTINEL NODE BIOPSIES:\u000aEXTENSIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia is present.\u000aTwo (2) sentinel nodes are negative for carcinoma. By report,\u000aimmunohistochemical studies for cytokeratin were examined.\u000aSkin with no significant pathologic change.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left breast DCIS, residual calcifications, MRI densities on lateral\u000aaspect\u000aClinical Diagnosis: DCIS and LCIS\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aConsult slides.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aReceived from Massachusetts General Hospital, Pathology Department, Boston, MA\u000aREMOVED_CASE_ID, are sixty-two (62) glass slides.\u000aTwenty-two (22) slides are labeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and sublabeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through\u000a"REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID"\u000aand "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", corresponding to A) left breast wire needle\u000alocalized excision (7.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) B) left breast, inferior, excision (3.0\u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) C) left breast, medial, excision (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) D) left\u000abreast, superior, excision (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) E) left breast, deep, excision\u000a(3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) F) left breast, lateral, excision (3.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000aperformed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying pathology report bearing\u000athe patient's name.\u000aForty (40) slides are labeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and sublabeled "FX#1", "FX#1A",\u000a"REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "FX#2", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "FX#3", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and\u000a"REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", corresponding to FX#1,FX#1A-A) right axillary nodes,\u000ahot and blue, #543 and #468, excision (two nodes: REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, and REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.9 x REMOVED_DATE cm) FX#2-B) left axillary sentinel node, hot and blue, #2903 and\u000a#660, excision (0.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) FX#3-C) left axillary sentinel lymph node,\u000ablue, excision (1.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) D) right breast simple mastectomy (23.8 x\u000a17.7 x REMOVED_DATE cm) E) left breast simple mastectomy (24.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000aperformed on REMOVED_DATE  according to the accompanying pathology report bearing\u000athe patient's name.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a2/7/2007\u000a[report_end]
