VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, partial mastectomy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Martin C Chang, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aNo residual invasive or in situ carcinoma identified.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aStromal fibrosis, chronic inflammation, and foreign body giant cell\u000areaction consistent with prior excision.\u000aStromal dystrophic calcification and arterial medial calcification\u000a("Monckeberg-type").\u000aOne (1) axillary lymph node; negative for tumor.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 65 y.o. female hx atypical ductal hyperplasia with small focus of\u000ainvasive L breast ca on bx\u000aOperation: L simple mastectomy\u000aOperative Findings: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis: L breast ca + atypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. Left breast (stitch @ axilla)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "#1 left breast (stitch at axilla)", and consists of 198-g simple\u000amastectomy specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. At the anterior surface is\u000aa REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm tan-brown skin ellipse, occupied almost entirely by areola (3.2\u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm) and nipple (everted, REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter). The specimen is oriented\u000awith a suture designating the axilla. The deep margin consists of a fascial\u000asurface, and is inked black. At the 11 o'clock edge of the specimen is a REMOVED_DATE x\u000a2.3 x REMOVED_DATE cm biopsy cavity with focal fat necrosis, widely abutting deep\u000amargin, and both superior and anterior edges. The remainder of the specimen is\u000acomposed 90% of fibrous tissue, including cyst formation, and 10% adipose\u000atissue. Two potential lymph nodes (0.6 and REMOVED_DATE cm) are identified in the\u000aadipose tissue designated at the axilla.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: nipple, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: breast tissue subjacent to nipple, multiple frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: upper inner quadrant, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lower inner quadrant, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: upper outer quadrant, 2 frags, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lower outer quadrant, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: axillary adipose tissue/potential lymph nodes, 2 frags, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: biopsy cavity and adjacent tissue, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMCC/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Martin C Chang M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aPrelim Dx Release by Martin C Chang M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aNo Action - Prelim Sent by Russell K Dorer M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
