VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST BIOPSY W/O MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aCORLESS, CHRISTOPHER, LEE, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by CORLESS,CHRISTOPHER LEE,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, NEEDLE-DIRECTED BIOPSY:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN-SITU, microscopic foci (block #6).\u000aNo invasive tumor identified.\u000aFibrocystic changes, proliferative, with associated microcalcifications.\u000aFibroadenoma (0.4 cm).\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "AXILLARY NODE":\u000aMETASTATIC ADENOCARCINOMA, present in a lymph node, with extra-nodal\u000aextension, see note.\u000aThe largest dimension of tumor in the node is REMOVED_DATE cm.\u000aNote: The tumor in this lymph node is composed of medium-sized cells\u000aarranged in sheets and small nests. Despite the absence of ductal tumor in\u000athe above biopsy, the histologic features favor a ductal over a lobular\u000aprimary.\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM: by CORLESS,CHRISTOPHER LEE,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aImmunoperoxidase stains performed on the axillary lymph node tissue (specimen\u000a#2, block #10) reveal the tumor cells to be strongly positive for gross cystic\u000adisease fluid protein (GCDFP), a finding that confirms a breast primary for\u000athis tumor.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Left needle localization biopsy and axillary biopsy.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1. Left breast tissue (F.S).\u000a#2. Axillary lymph node (lymphoma markers).\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aLEFT BREAST BIOPSY:\u000aNo areas grossly suspicious for infiltrating carcinoma. Single nodule\u000a(0.4 cm), grossly consistent with fibroadenoma.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcium in specimen.,\u000aT. FRENNA ,M.D\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by HASEGAWA,SUSAN LYNN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in two parts, both labelled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aThe first part of the specimen, labelled "breast biopsy", consists of one\u000afragment of yellow/tan fibrofatty tissue (4.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) containing a\u000aneedle localization wire in place. The breast tissue is composed mainly of\u000afibrofatty and contains a single nodule (0.4 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is grossly\u000aconsistent with a fibroadenoma. There are no areas that are grossly\u000asuspicious for invasive carcinoma. A specimen radiograph was performed and\u000abreast fragments containing microcalcifications were submitted as noted below.\u000aMicro #1: Breast with microcalcification, ESS, 2 frags, 1 cass.\u000aMicros #2-3: Other calcified areas by specimen radiograph, ESS, 5 frags, 2\u000acass.\u000aMicros #4-9: Remainder of breast biopsy, ESS, 11 frags, 6 cass.\u000aThe second specimen, labelled "axillary node", consists of a 3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afragment of fibrofatty with a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm encapsulated hemorrhagic area and a\u000a0.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm gray gritty area. A portion of the tissue has been saved for snap\u000afreeze and possibly ERICA analysis.\u000aMicro #10: Axillary node, 1 frag, 1 cass.\u000aReports to: MATORY, YVEDT M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST BIOPSY W/O MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: LYMPH NODE FOR HEMATOPATHOLOGY WORKUP\u000a[report_end]
