VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Scott Lovitch, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP MARKING A CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY\u000aSITE:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, is present as multiple small foci (all\u000a< REMOVED_DATE cm), located adjacent to the core needle biopsy site (blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; levels examined). Calcifications are associated with ADH. No necrosis is\u000aseen.\u000aThe ADH is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest medial margin.\u000aNote: The patient had a core needle biopsy for calcifications that showed ADH\u000a(REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The ADH in the excision has the identical appearance, consisting\u000aof a cribriform population of monomorphic cells with low grade nuclei. The\u000adifferential diagnosis would include a very small area of low grade DCIS.\u000aHowever, classification as ADH is favored due to the very limited extent.\u000aThe entire specimen was examined microscopically.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester, who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 58 y/o female whose REMOVED_DATE  mammogram demonstrated right subareolar\u000acalcifications. REMOVED_DATE  stereotactic core biopsy resealed ADH closely\u000aapproaching DCIS, excision is recommended.\u000aOperation: R breast wire-localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Rt breast wire-localized biopsy (to mammo); inks per path; stitches:\u000ashort = superior; long = lateral; whip stitch = deep margin.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip in specimen. Called to Dr. Rhei by Dr. Yeh. 1:40 P.M.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000amedical record number, and "#1. Right breast wire localized biopsy stitches\u000ashort equals superior, long equals lateral, whipstitch equals deep margin". It\u000aconsists of a single wire localized breast excision specimen with three\u000adesignating sutures (short equals superior, long equals lateral, whipstitch\u000aequals posterior), measuring REMOVED_DATE cm (superior to inferior) x REMOVED_DATE cm (anterior\u000ato posterior) x REMOVED_DATE cm (lateral to medial). The specimen is previously inked\u000aby the surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. A wire enters the specimen superiorly. The specimen is\u000aaccompanied by a radiograph showing a clip associated with the wire in the\u000aspecimen. The clip appears to be located in inferolateral aspect of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is bisected along the hook wire. There is a tan, firm\u000aarea, consistent with possible biopsy site, which is associated with a clip and\u000ameasures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The core biopsy site is located REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aclosest superior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest\u000aposterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest anterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest lateral,\u000aand REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest medial margin. The entire specimen is submitted\u000afrom superior to inferior.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Specimen serially sectioned from superior to inferior with\u000apossible biopsy cavity in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and area of clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, multi frags, ESS.\u000aSEK/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
