VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, SIMPLE MASTECTOMY:\u000aINVASIVE CARCINOMA AND DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU WITH MICROINVASION.\u000aInvasive tubular carcinoma, well differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade I/III), REMOVED_DATE cm, is present in the upper outer quadrant (block 15).\u000aThis carcinoma appears to be an incidental finding and is not associated\u000awith calcifications. An immunoperoxidase study (for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) confirms the\u000aabsence of myoepithelial cells, supporting this diagnosis. The carcinoma\u000ais positive for estrogen and progesterone receptors and negative for\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu.\u000aDCIS, comedo, solid, and cribriform types (high nuclear grade), with\u000acentral necrosis and calcifications, is extensively present at the 6:00\u000aposition (corresponding to the clinical palpable mass and associated with\u000aa core needle biopsy site), in a large duct below the nipple, and in the\u000aupper outer quadrant, associated with radiologic calcifications.\u000aMICROINVASION (< REMOVED_DATE cm) is present as, at least, two foci associated with\u000athe DCIS (block 3). Immunoperoxidase studies confirm the absence of\u000amyoepithelial cells in the areas of microinvasion (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). The DCIS and the\u000aareas of microinvasion are negative for estrogen and progesterone\u000areceptors and positive for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (3+).\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU, is present.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is present.\u000aA remote surgical biopsy site is present in the upper outer quadrant.\u000aThe deep margin is not involved.\u000aThe skin and nipple are not involved.\u000aNote: The patient appears to have two separate breast carcinomas,\u000aprobably biologically unrelated given the difference in histologic\u000aappearance and immunophenotype.\u000aNo lymph nodes were found in the lateral tissue of the breast after an\u000aextensive gross and microscopic examination of this tissue.\u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa NX MX.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 54 year old female with ADH and DCIS - I'm worried about an invasive\u000acomponent at\u000a6:00 just below areola. Took low axillary nodes (3-5 or so,\u000aprobable).\u000aOperation: Left simple mastectomy with tissue expander\u000aClinical Diagnosis: DCIS left breast\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1. Left breast - stitch axilla\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by WOLGAMOT,GREGORY M.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number and "#1. left breast - stitch axilla", and consists of a simple\u000amastectomy specimen (127g; 15 x 10 x REMOVED_DATE cm overall). The specimen contains a\u000askin ellipse (7.5 x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a somewhat indistinct areola (2.5 cm diameter)\u000aand a nipple (1.4 cm diameter). No cutaneous scars are evident. The deep\u000afascial plane, which is glistening smooth and slides easily over the specimen,\u000ais inked in black.\u000aThe specimen is radiographed, and the radiograph reveals multiple small\u000acalcifications in the upper outer quadrant, a large calcification medial to\u000athe nipple, and a density inferior to the nipple.\u000aThe breast is composed of approximately 70% white fibrous tissue and 30%\u000aadipose tissue. Approximately 2 cm inferior to the nipple is a firm white mass\u000a(1.5 cm) with circumscribed borders. The mass has a central cavitation (0.4 cm\u000adiameter) consistent with a prior biopsy site. A hemorrhage tract extends from\u000athis region medially, associated with fat necrosis. The firm mass is\u000aapproximately REMOVED_DATE cm from the deep margin. No other masses are identified in\u000athe specimen. The tissue in the lateral portion is fixed in Bouin's and\u000athinly sectioned. No definite lymph nodes are identified. The tissue is\u000aextensively sampled.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  mass at 6 o'clock, 1 frag each, ESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  fibrous tissue adjacent to mass, 1 frag each, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  UOQ, region of calcifications, 1 frag each, RSS\u000aMicro 16: large calcification, 2 frags ESS\u000aMicro 17: nipple, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro 18: superomedial quadrant, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro 19: inferomedial quadrant near mass and biopsy site, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro 20: inferolateral quadrant, 1 frag, RSS\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  lateral breast tissue, mult frags, RSS\u000agmw\u000as, RSS\u000agmw\u000aReports to: IGLEHART, JAMES, DIRK M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST MASTECTOMY - PARTIAL OR SIMPLE\u000a[report_end]
