VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kremena V. Star, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Gilbert Brodsky, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, TISSUE:\u000aBreast tissue with atypical ductal hyperplasia, involving one of four\u000a(1:4) blocks (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), with associated calcifications.\u000aCysts, apocrine metaplasia, and columnar alteration.\u000aCalcifications associated with benign breast tissue.\u000aMultiple levels examined.\u000aMargins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: Dr. Jane Brock, Breast Pathology Service, has reviewed this case and\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 53-year-old premenopausal female whose SSH REMOVED_DATE  May and REMOVED_DATE -\u000aMay views demonstrate left breast anterior/medial calcifications for which\u000abiopsy is recommended, not amenable to core biopsy. There are additional faint\u000aupper inner quadrant calcifications for which 6 month F/U mammogram is\u000arecommended.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue - short stitch superior, long stitch lateral; Whip\u000astitch on chest wall. (Mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY COMMENT:\u000aSpecimen contains ca++ and intact wire. Called to Dr. Rhei by Dr. Lizer at\u000a11:10 am.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000ahe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Left breast tissue short stitch superior long lateral, whip stitch\u000achest wall margin", and consists of a ragged REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures designating superior (short suture),\u000alateral (long suture), and chest wall (whip stitch) margins. The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange\u000a(inked by surgeon). Localizing wire enters inferior/posterior surface and\u000aexits anterior/superior surface. The specimen is received with an accompanying\u000aradiograph demonstrating a localizing wire in place within the specimen, which\u000aexhibits peripheral focal calcifications. The specimen is serially sectioned\u000afrom medial to lateral, revealing ____ tan/white fibrous tissue (at least 90%),\u000awithout palpable or visible masses, lesions or other abnormalities grossly.\u000aEntire specimen is sequentially submitted from medial to lateral, with\u000aperpendicular medial margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, perpendicular lateral margin in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000adb\u005cmgd\u005cpab\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on Friday May\u000a08, 2009 at 05:59:30PM\u000a[report_end]
