ack; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral,\u000aexhibiting an ill-defined, white, firm, cystic mass (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which\u000ais traversed by the needle-localization wire. The cystic mass abuts the\u000aposterior, anterior, and superior margins, comes to within REMOVED_DATE cm of the\u000ainferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm of the medial margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm of the lateral\u000amargins. The accompanying radiograph confirms the presence of a density with a\u000aneedle-localization wire in place. Note: An additional free floating wire\u000a(2.0 cm in length) is present within the container.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial aspect of the breast specimen to include lesion, 4 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral portion of breast tissue, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "left fibrocystic breast tissue, lateral to specimen #1", consists of\u000athree fragments of unoriented yellow/white fibroadipose soft tissue (ranging\u000afrom REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm up to REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Each fragment is inked\u000ablack and sectioned, exhibiting approximately 95% tan/white dense, focally\u000acystic stroma and 5% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The specimen was\u000aaccompanied by a radiograph exhibiting two fragments of tissue with no\u000alocalized densities or lesions specified by radiology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Small fragment of fibroadipose tissue, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Largest fragment of fibroadipose tissue (trisected), 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Fragment fibroadipose tissue, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aKG:lc\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by George L Mutter M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
