VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST BIOPSY W/O MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBRENN, THOMAS, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BRENN,THOMAS,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, LUMPECTOMY:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (block #12).\u000aColumnar change with flat atypia.\u000aIntraductal papillomas with associatead duct ectasia; acute and chronic\u000ainflammation with foreign body giant cell reaction, and reactive\u000afibrosis.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aThis case was reviewed with Dr. A. Richardson who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Hx, R lymph d/c.\u000aOperation: R nipple exploration & d/c.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: R nipple d/c.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: 1. R breast tissue, short superior, long lateral.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by SHOLL,LYNETTE MARIE,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber and "#1 R breast tissue", and consists of one yellow/tan lumpectomy\u000aspecimen (6.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a short suture indicating superior and long\u000asuture indicating lateral. There is a candidate portion of breast ductule (0.7\u000acm in length x REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter) protruding from the anterior/inferior\u000amargin. There is an additional candidate portion of breast ductule (0.6 x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm in diameter) protruding from the superior/posterior aspect of the specimen.\u000aThe candidate ductal margins are taken en face and entirely submitted. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned to reveal an area of white/tan fibrous tissue\u000a(2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), surrounding a likely ectatic duct (0.5 cm in maximum\u000adiameter). The fibrous tissue grossly extends to the superior margin, to the\u000ainferior margin, to the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE \u000acm from the lateral margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin. The remainder\u000aof the breast tissue is composed of approximately 5% fibrous tissue, the rest\u000ais adipose tissue.\u000aMicro 1: en face sections of candidate duct margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 2: inferior, medial, posterior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 3: inferior and anterior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 4: superior and posterior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 5: duct lesion with surrounding fibrous tissue and inferior and\u000asuperior margins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 6: duct lesion and surrounding superior, anterior, inferior, posterior\u000amargins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 7: duct lesion and surrounding superior, anterior, posterior, inferior\u000amargins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 8: duct lesion and closest superior, anterior, inferior, posterior\u000amargins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 9: duct lesion with posterior and inferior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 10: duct lesion with closest posterior, inferior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 11: duct lesion with closest posterior and inferior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 12: duct lesion with posterior and inferior margins, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 13: inferior and posterior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 14: posterior and inferior margins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 15: posterior, lateral, inferior margins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 16: posterior and inferior margins, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 17: lateral, superior, posterior margin, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 18: lateral and posterior margins, 2 frags, ESS.\u000ajdr\u000aReports to: SHOJI, BRENT, T. M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST BIOPSY W/O MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
