VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Dominique P. Coco, M.D.\u000aPathologist: James L Connolly, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSENTINEL LYMPH NODE#1:\u000aOne lymph node, negative for tumor (0/1).\u000aSENTINEL LYMPH NODE#2:\u000aSeven lymph nodes, negative for tumor (0/7).\u000aNON-SENTINEL NODES:\u000aTwo lymph nodes, negative for tumor (0/2).\u000aRIGHT BREAST, RE-EXCISION (SUPERIOR HEMISPHERE):\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU within and adjacent to previous excision site,\u000apresent in 7 of 10 blocks.\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, focally present in 1 of 10 blocks.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, RE-EXCISION (INFEIOR HEMISPHERE):\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU within and adjacent to previous excision site,\u000apresent in 9 of 9 blocks.\u000aNote: In both excisions, extensive lobular carcinoma in-situ is present but the\u000aDCIS (low and intermediate nuclear grades) reported in the previous excision is\u000anot identified. A single focus of ADH is identified in block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: R breast cancer - CIS with mixed ductal and lobular features and\u000amicroinvasion.\u000aOperation: R partial mastectomy, R sentinel node biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA. Sentinel node #1 count 190, 2 nodes.\u000aB. Sentinal node #2 count 430.\u000aC. Non sentinel nodes.\u000aD. Superior hemisphere global re-excision.\u000aE. Inferior hemisphere global re-excision.\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aArrival time: 10:30 AM\u000aReport time: 10:55 AM\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#1. SENTINEL NODE #1 COUNT 190" (FSA):\u000aOne (1) lymph node; negative for tumor.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#2. SENTINEL NODE #2 COUNT 430" (FSB):\u000aTwo (2) lymph node; negative for tumor.\u000aOR Consultation by: DAVID W. KINDELBERGER, M.D.\u000aResident: TANYA REGE, M.D., PH.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnosis(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh and in five parts labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Sentinel node #1, greater than 2 nodes, count 190", consists of\u000aone tan/pink soft tissue fragment (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is\u000abisected and entirely submitted in the frozen section room as FSA.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: FSA remnant, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Sentinel node #2, counts 430", consists of one tan/pink soft\u000atissue fragment (3.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The lymph nodes are submitted in the\u000afrozen section room as REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID remnant, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remaining adipose tissue, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart C, "#3. Non-sentinel node", consists of two fragments of tan/yellow\u000afibroadipose tissue (2.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE  REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Two lymph nodes are\u000aidentified (1.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Larger lymph node, bisected, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Smaller lymph node, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart D, "#4. Superior hemisphere, global re-excision", consists one breast\u000alumpectomy specimen (16 g, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). No orienting sutures are\u000apresent. Inks by surgeon. The breast parenchyma is tan/white and fibrotic.\u000aGreater than 95% of the breast is fibrotic and the areas of fibrous are extend\u000ato all inked margins.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections of breast from medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE frags each,\u000aESS.\u000aPart E, "#5. Inferior hemispheric global re-excision", consists of one breast\u000alumpectomy (14 g, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). Specimen is received unoriented. Inks\u000aby surgeon. A firm tan/white cystic nodule (0.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) is present,\u000a0.3 cm from the posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial\u000amargin and REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin. Remainder of the breast parenchyma\u000ais unremarkable.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior hemisphere, medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS.\u000aThe mass is present in cassettes REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aTR/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by James L Connolly M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a10/11/2007\u000a[report_end]
