nters through the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits through the\u000amedial aspect of the specimen. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol\u000a(superior red, inferior green, medial orange, lateral yellow, anterior blue,\u000aand posterior black), and is serially sectioned from medial to lateral. The\u000acut surface is tan-yellow and homogenous with focal areas of dense white\u000afibrous tissue constituting less than 2% of the breast parenchyma. The\u000aspecimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections medial to lateral blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to\u000ainclude possible areas of microcalcifications per specimen radiograph, adjacent\u000ato needle localization wire, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aSEK/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Gilbert Brodsky M.D., Electronically signed on Thursday\u000aMay 07, 2009 at 04:09:07PM\u000a[report_end]
