VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aKRANE, JEFFREY, FRED, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by KRANE,JEFFREY FRED,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aFocal atypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid spread (block 5).\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 49 year old premenopausal female with right breast UOQ\u000acalcifications with attempted core biopsy REMOVED_DATE  yielding benign breast tissue\u000aand vascular calcifications.\u000aOperation: Right breast wire localization\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal right mammogram\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Right breast wire localized biopsy\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcifications in specimen (JL).\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by OH,KIRSTINE YUNG-TING,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number and "#1. right breast wire localized medial lateral excision". The\u000aspecimen is also received with a mammogram showing a breast mass with a soft\u000atissue density located at one border of the specimen measuring approximately\u000a1.2 cm in diameter from the mammogram. In addition, adjacent to the soft\u000atissue density there are multiple calcifications (largest REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter),\u000alocated REMOVED_DATE cm away from the soft tissue density. The specimen is received\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior = black;\u000aanterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow; medial =\u000aorange. The specimen is received with a wire localizing needle specimen.\u000aSerial sectioning reveals a white fibrous area (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), and\u000aadjacent yellow and red hemorrhagic fatty area. The solid area comprises of\u000aapproximately 30% of volume of the specimen.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue, 2 or 1 frag each, ESS\u000ako\u000aReports to: KAELIN, CAROLYN, MARY M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
