VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Samuel G Katz, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Michelle S Hirsch, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST TISSUE":\u000aBreast tissue with atypical ductal hyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis, dense\u000afibrosis, and calcifications associated with benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Hx of calcification, left breast. Pt requested excisional biopsy\u000a(Area has not been cored.).\u000aOperation: Left mammographically-directed breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aLeft breast tissue, short=superior, long=lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aCalcs in specimen - jm139\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "Left breast tissue", and consists of one fragment of fibroadipose tissue\u000awith two, orientating sutures and a wire in place (2.8 cm superior to inferior\u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral x REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior). Accompanying the\u000aspecimen is a radiograph and a report that reads, "Calcifications in specimen".\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The inferior border of the specimen is composed mostly of\u000awhite, fibrous tissue. Please note that the two, orientating sutures appear to\u000abe near the same surface. The specimen is, therefore, reviewed in the grossing\u000aroom with Dr. Dillon. The specimen is sliced into six slices, from superior to\u000ainferior, with the wire (and calcifications) located within slices 5 and 6.\u000aThe breast parenchyma is comprised of approximately 60% white fibrous tissue\u000athat is located within the inferior portion of the specimen. The remaining\u000abreast parenchyma is yellow, adipose tissue. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior, anterior, medial and lateral margins of slice 2, 1\u000afrag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial, posterior and anterior margins of slice 3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and lateral margins of slice 3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial, posterior and anterior margins of slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and lateral margins of slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and medial margins of slice 5, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior, posterior and lateral margins of slice 5, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicros REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, perpendicular sections (near the wire), 3 frags\u000aeach,\u000aESS.\u000asak/MMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Samuel G Katz M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michelle S Hirsch M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a2/1/2005\u000a[report_end]
