VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Sentinel Lymph Nodes(s)\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Andrew G. Evans, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, DOUBLE WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A BIOPSY SITE, A CLIP AND\u000aMICROCALCIFICATIONS AND SENTINEL NODE BIOPSIES (THREE SPECIMENS, INCLUDING\u000aFROZEN SECTIONS):\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, moderately differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade II/III) (tubule score=2, nuclear score=3, mitoses score=1, total=6), is\u000apresent as two foci, each REMOVED_DATE cm in size (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; deeper levels; block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aTwo additional foci of MICROINVASION are present, < REMOVED_DATE cm in size (slides REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aand REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is present.\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid and cribriform types (high nuclear grades),\u000awith necrosis and microcalcifications, is associated with and away from the\u000ainvasive carcinoma (13 of 41 blocks; EIC positive).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia is present (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aA core needle biopsy site is present.\u000aThe superior, inferior, medial, lateral, anterior, and posterior margins are\u000anegative for invasive carcinoma, including the foci of microinvasion.\u000aDCIS is multifocally present < REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior, medial, and lateral\u000amargins. The superior, anterior, and posterior margins are negative for DCIS\u000a(> REMOVED_DATE cm).\u000aTwo sentinel lymph nodes are negative for carcinoma (3 H&E levels for each\u000alymph node are examined).\u000aNote: The patient underwent a prior core needle biopsy for\u000acalcifications which revealed DCIS with microinvasion that was positive for\u000aER and PR and negative for REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu. Immunoperoxidase studies will be\u000aperformed on the larger area of invasion in this excision and reported in\u000aan addendum.\u000aThis carcinoma would be classified as AJCC (6th edition): REMOVED_ACCESSION_IDa REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID (sn)\u000awith additional foci of invasion.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 58 year old female postmenopausal with family history of 2 paternal\u000aaunts with breast cancer, personal risk factors of nulliparity and history of\u000aADH. REMOVED_DATE  screening mammogram demonstrates L calcifications. REMOVED_DATE \u000astereotactic core biopsy revealed comedo DCIS with microinvasion.\u000aOperation: L breast wire-localized lumpectomy with 2 bracketing wires, L\u000asentinel lymph node mapping, biopsy and possible axillary dissection.\u000aPREVIOUS SURGERY: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aOperative Findings: None provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: L breast DCIS wide excision.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left sentinel node hot and blue (F.S.)\u000aB/2) Left sentinel node hot not blue (F.S.).\u000aC/3) Left breast tissue; short stitch - superior; long - lateral, whip stitch\u000achest wall margin (mammo).\u000aO.R. CONSULTATION:\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#1. LEFT SENTINEL NODE HOT AND BLUE" (FSA):\u000aOne lymph node, negative for tumor.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "#2. LEFT SENTINEL NODE HOT NOT BLUE" (FSB):\u000aOne lymph node, negative for tumor.\u000aOR Consultation by: Alessandra F. Nascimento, M.D.\u000aThe senior physician certifies that he/she personally conducted a gross and/or\u000amicroscopic examination of the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed\u000athe rapid diagnos(es) related thereto.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in three parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, medical record number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left sentinel node, hot and blue". The specimen consists of one\u000ayellow fibroadipose tissue (2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) containing a single lymph node\u000a(1.7 cm in greatest dimension), which was bisected and entirely submitted for\u000afrozen section examination as FSA.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: FSA, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Non-frozen tissue, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Left sentinel node, hot not blue". The specimen consists of one\u000ayellow fibroadipose tissue fragment (2.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) containing one lymph\u000anode (0.9 cm in greatest dimension), which was bisected and entirely submitted\u000afor frozen section examination as FSB.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: FSB, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Non-frozen tissue, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart C, "#3. Left breast tissue, short stitch superior, long lateral,\u000awhip-stitch chest wall margin". The specimen consists of a single portion of\u000aadipose tissue (5.8 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, and REMOVED_DATE \u000acm anterior to posterior), which is oriented by a short stitch on the superior\u000aaspect of the specimen, a long stitch on the lateral aspect of the specimen,\u000aand a whip-stitch on the posterior aspect of the specimen, which is designated\u000aby the surgeon as chest wall margin. Two wire needles are protruding from the\u000aspecimen, one from the lateral superior aspect of the specimen and the other\u000afrom the medial inferior aspect of the specimen. The tissue is accompanied by\u000aa radiograph, which demonstrates a clip in an area of irregular density\u000aconsistent with biopsy cavity, calcifications, and two hookwires present on\u000aopposite side aspects of the specimen. The accompanying report reads "calcs\u000aclip and two wires. Called to OR by Lisa Zorn on REMOVED_DATE  at 3:50 P.M. to\u000aDr. Rhei". The wire at the inferior medial aspect of the specimen inserts\u000awithin an area of focal hemorrhage and diffuse irregular nodularity (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm). Within this area at least three firm white calcified nodules (0.2\u000acm in greatest dimension) are identified. This area is within REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000amedial margin and REMOVED_DATE cm to the inferior margin, and greater than REMOVED_DATE cm to\u000aeach remaining margin. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The wire entering from the\u000asuperior lateral aspect of the breast, terminates at the anterior inferior\u000aportion (designated wire #2) traversing in area of breast tissue extensively\u000amarked with blue ink. A focal nodularity (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) is found within\u000athe center the specimen, REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior margin, the nearest\u000aresection margin. The second focal nodularity (0.9 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) is found\u000awithin the anterior aspect of the specimen, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial resection\u000amargin. At the inferior surface of the specimen, focal firm nodules and white\u000afirm tissue covering an area of REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm is identified.\u000aApproximately 60% of the specimen is submitted for microscopic analysis, and\u000athe remaining tissue is all unremarkable fibroadipose tissue. The cut sections\u000aand the non-submitted tissues are both radiographed for subsequent\u000aidentification of the clip.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior/medial areas of calcifications and nodularity, 1 frag\u000aeach, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Adjacent areas examining the medial posterior and inferior margin,\u000aincluding clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Area of nodularity adjacent to posterior margin, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Areas of nodularity adjacent to the medial margin, 1 frag each,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Firm/nodule/calcified areas at the superior aspect of the\u000aspecimen, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Areas of firmness near the inferior aspect, 1 frag each, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Additional tissue from the superior aspect of the specimen, 1 frag,\u000aRSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remaining tissue, mult frags, ESS.\u000aAEV/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on Friday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 15, 2010 at 11:10:49PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (0)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a< 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000aMonday REMOVED_MONTH 18, 2010 at 12:06:52PM\u000a[report_end]
