VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: Esther Rhei M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-04-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident\u000aPAULA C. ABREU-E-LIMA, M.D.\u000aPathologist\u000aSHUJI OGINO, M.D., PH.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST EXCISION WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP (1\u000aSPECIME):\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000aSYNOPSIS:\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU - Present\u000aTypes - Cribriform, micropapillary\u000aNuclear grade - Low to intermediate\u000aCalcifications in DCIS - Present\u000aCentral necrosis in DCIS - Absent\u000aMicroinvasion (<0.1cm) - Absent\u000aExtent of DCIS\u000aNumber of blocks/total REMOVED_DATE blocks (#REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aFinal Margins (DCIS)\u000aNegative (>0.3 cm) Medial, inferior, anterior, superior,\u000aPosterior\u000aClose (0.1 cm) Lateral (multifocal, blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID)\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU - Absent\u000aOTHER FINDINGS Atypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid\u000aSpread\u000aSclerosing adenosis.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aAJCC CLASSIFICATION (6th edition) - Tis pNx MX\u000aSPECIAL STUDIES (ER/PR/HER-2) - Pending\u000aNOTE: The majority of calcifications are seen in association with sclerosing\u000aadenosis. The DCIS is located in the lateral portion of the specimen, adjcent\u000ato the site of the recent core needle biopsy. A focus of ADH with some features\u000aof this patient's DCIS is present less than REMOVED_DATE cm from the postero-lateral\u000amargin (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aThe case has been reviewed by Dr. D. Dillon, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 74-y-o with R abn/mammo REMOVED_DATE  25-cm of calcs R UOQ.\u000aCore bx REMOVED_DATE  --> atypical lobular hyperplasia, ADH.\u000aPrior R breast bx REMOVED_DATE  --> ADH.\u000aOperation: R breast wire loc bx.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right abnormal mammogram.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Biopsy, clip and calcification clusters contained in specimen\u000a(clipped marked site of ADH biopsy.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. Right breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral, whip\u000astitch deep margin.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name and unit number\u000aand "1. Right breast tissue, short stitch superior, long stitch lateral, whip\u000astitch deep margin". It consists of a previously inked breast excision\u000aspecimen (5.1 from medial to lateral, x REMOVED_DATE from superior to inferior, x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom anterior to posterior), containing a long and a short stitch designated as\u000alateral and superior, respectively. No whip stitch is identified. Within the\u000aspecimen is a single localization wire, which penetrates the specimen through\u000athe lateral margin and exits through the posterior margin. The accompanying\u000aspecimen radiograph shows multiple microcalcifications, adjacent to which are\u000athe localization wire and a single clip. Within the specimen is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.2 cm white-grey fibrotic area, corresponding to where the wire was placed. It\u000agrossly abuts the lateral margin, and is REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe superior, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin. No\u000ablue ink (anterior margin) is identified in the specimen. No discrete masses\u000aare grossly seen. The specimen is serially sectioned and submitted from\u000alateral to medial. The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections from lateral to medial, Micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID containing the\u000afibrotic area with microcalcifications, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000apal/MMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Paula C Abreu-e-Lima M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aMicroscopic/Diagnostic Dictation by Paula C Abreu-e-Lima M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Shuji Ogino M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a12/31/2004\u000aFIRST ADDENDUM REMOVED_DATE  Dr. Deborah Dillon\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for in situ carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) POSITIVE (2+)\u000aInterpretation:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is assessed visually and reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low >0% to <10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not yet\u000abeen determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aPositive (2+): Weak to moderate complete membrane\u000aimmunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+): Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity\u000ain >10% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aSome of the reagents used in these assays may be approved for investigational\u000ause only.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
