VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Revised Report **********\u000aResident: Christopher P. Elco, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Gilbert Brodsky, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aSPECIMEN DESIGNATED "WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY, RIGHT BREAST":\u000aBenign breast parenchyma with focal atypical ductal hyperplasia,\u000acolumnar cell change, fibroadenoma, sclerosing adenosis, complex\u000asclerosing lesion with epithelial hyperplasia, cysts with apocrine\u000ametaplasia, core biopsy site, and calcifications in association with\u000abenign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aNOTE: Immunohistochemical double stain for p63 and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID confirms the\u000apresence of myoepithelial cells in areas of tubular ducts within the complex\u000asclerosing lesion.\u000aCOMMENT: This report represents an amended diagnosis for technical details\u000aonly. In the original report, the components ("p63 and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID") of the "double\u000astain" were not given. The diagnosis is otherwise unchanged. (Dr. Rhei notified\u000aby email from Dr. Brodsky REMOVED_DATE  at 10:10 am.)\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 53 year old peri menopausal woman, s/p REMOVED_DATE  R breast core biopsies\u000a- UIQ revealing ADH associated with calcifications - exc recommended\u000aOperation: R. breast WL biopsy\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided\u000aClinical Diagnosis: See above\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Wire-localized excisional biopsy, R breast\u000aTagged: short/superior; long/lateral; whip/deep\u000aRADIOLOGY COMMENT:\u000aCa+/clip/wire in specimen. Called to Dr. Rhei by [illegible signature]\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Wire localized excisional biopsy", and consists of a REMOVED_DATE (medial to\u000alateral) x REMOVED_DATE (superior to inferior) x REMOVED_DATE (anterior to posterior) cm breast\u000abiopsy with a localization wire in place and a short suture marking the\u000asuperior margin, a long suture marking the lateral margin, and a whip stitch\u000adesignating the deep margin. An accompanying radiograph demonstrates diffuse\u000acalcifications in the central portion of the specimen along with a clip and an\u000aadjacent localization wire. The specimen is received inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The inferior and\u000asuperior margins are removed to be submitted perpendicularly and the remainder\u000aof the specimen is sectioned into five slices from superior to inferior. There\u000ais an ill-defined lesion/white firm area (2.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) extending to the\u000amedial and anterior borders in slice #1 and to the posterior border in slices\u000a#3 through #5. The mass is REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral border, and focally abuts\u000athe inferior and superior borders. The remaining breast parenchyma is composed\u000aof unremarkable yellow adipose tissue. The specimen is submitted in its\u000aentirety for histological examination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, perpendicular sections, REMOVED_DATE frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #1, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro 