VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Adam Rosendorff, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED LUMPECTOMY FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aFocal atypical ductal hyperplasia (0.8 mm).\u000aSparse calcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aentire specimen, including margins.\u000aB. ADDITIONAL TISSUE, LEFT BREAST:\u000aBreast tissue with calcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aentire specimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 48 year old female with abnormal left breast calcifications.\u000aOperation: L breast wire localized lumpectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast lumpectomy - sent to mammo (long stitch lateral, short stitch\u000asuperior).\u000aB/2 Additional left breast tissue - mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY: 1. Left breast lumpectomy: A few scattered calcifications and intact\u000ahook wire within the specimen. 2. Additional tissue, left breast:\u000aCalcifications are within the specimen. Dr. Gombos.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Left breast lumpectomy, stitch long lateral, short superior", and\u000aconsists of an irregular portion of fibrofatty tissue (4.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm),\u000awith a long suture designating lateral, and a short suture designating\u000asuperior. There is an inserted needle-localization wire, which enters along\u000athe anterior margin, and exits at the posterior margin. The specimen is inked\u000aper pathology protocol as anterior blue, posterior black, superior red,\u000ainferior green, lateral yellow and medial orange, and is serially sectioned\u000afrom medial to lateral to reveal 95% dense white fibrous tissue, and 5% yellow\u000alobulated adipose tissue, with no discrete masses or lesions identified. The\u000aspecimen is sequentially submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, radially, 2 frags each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, from medial to lateral, with the\u000apossible area of concern on the accompanying radiograph in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1\u000afrag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, radially, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Additional tissue, left breast", and consists of an unoriented\u000airregular portion of fibrofatty tissue (3.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The specimen is\u000ainked black and serially sectioned to reveal 99% dense white fibrous tissue,\u000aand 1% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, with no discrete masses identified.\u000aThe specimen is sequentially submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 1 unoriented end, radially, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Opposite unoriented end, radially, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000acp/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a10/16/2007\u000a[report_end]
