VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, partial mastectomy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Stephanie Schulte, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, SUBCUTANEOUS MASTECTOMY:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (best seen in blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aGynecomastia.\u000aNote: The entire specimen is examined microscopically, including margins. The\u000aatypical hyperplasia seen in the mastectomy is similar to that seen in the\u000aprior biopsy (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of melanoma in 1994.\u000aOperation: Left subcutaneous mastectomy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Atypical hyperplasia.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left subcutaneous mastectomy, suture long/lateral, suture short/superior.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name and unit number\u000aand "#1. Left subcutaneous mastectomy, suture long/lateral, suture\u000ashort/superior", and consists of a breast excision (5.5 cm from superior to\u000ainferior, REMOVED_DATE cm from medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior to posterior)\u000awith two orienting sutures (long-lateral, short-superior). The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe specimen is sectioned in 11 slices from superior to inferior. Slices #4\u000aand #5 contain an area of hemorrhage with adjacent fat necrosis (0.9 x REMOVED_DATE x\u000a0.5 cm), abutting the anterior margin and located REMOVED_DATE cm to medial, REMOVED_DATE cm to\u000alateral, REMOVED_DATE cm to posterior, REMOVED_DATE cm to superior and remote (more than REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000ato inferior. The remaining breast parenchyma is 20% composed of white fibrous\u000atissue, to 80% of yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The entire specimen is\u000aexamined microscopically.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, perpendicular, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #2, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #3, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #4 (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to include area of hemorrhage), 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #5 (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to include area of hemorrhage), 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #6, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #7, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #8, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #9, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice #10, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin, perpendicular, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aSTS/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: BS-07-REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a6/19/2007\u000a[report_end]
