VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BETH ANN LESNIKOSKI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Jason H Smouse, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, MRI-GUIDED WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA with apocrine features (0.15 cm focus; block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID;\u000a"anterior wire").\u000aApocrine adenosis (0.5 cm; block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; adjacent to "posterior wire").\u000aFibroadenomatoid changes.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aCalcifications present in association with benign epithelium.\u000aNOTE: Multiple levels of blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID were examined.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Status post radiation therapy, history of lymphoma, new bloody left\u000anipple discharge and segmental enhancement on MRI, left; rule out\u000apapillomatosis vs. malignancy.\u000aOperation: Left breast MRI-guided wire localization biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Fatty breast tissue.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Bloody nipple [illegible] abnormal MRI>\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue with wires x 2.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aTwo wires in specimen - taped wire should be to posterior enhancing lesion, but\u000aplease confirm by inked orientation of specimen.\u000aSignature: E.P. Frost, #REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number,\u000aand "#1 L breast tissue", and consists of an REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of\u000afibroadipose tissue, previously oriented with inks per surgeon per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The accompanying\u000aradiograph demonstrates two localizing wires in place within the specimen, an\u000aanterior and a posterior wire. The anterior wire is designated as being "marked\u000awith paper tape"; however, there is no marking tape attached to the wire, nor\u000ais it received within the specimen container. Localizing wires are identified\u000aas anterior and posterior with Drs. Dillon and Smouse of the Breast Pathology\u000aService, according to the morphology of the specimen in comparison to the\u000aaccompanying radiograph. The specimen is photographed. The anterior localizing\u000awire enters the inferior/lateral surface of the specimen, while the posterior\u000alocalizing wire enters the anterior/medial surface of the specimen and exits\u000asuperior/medially. The posterior wire is the more superior of the two.\u000aLocalizing wires are separated by approximately REMOVED_DATE cm. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned from superior to inferior. The tan/white fibrodense tissue\u000ain the area of the posterior localizing wire is slightly palpably firm;\u000ahowever, there are no identifiable masses, lesions or other abnormalities.\u000aTissue in the are of the anterior localizing wire is remarkable for a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm, tan/pink possible cyst (versus parenchymal nodule), REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aclosest anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the\u000aclosest posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe closest inferior margin, and remote (greater than REMOVED_DATE cm) from the closest\u000asuperior margin. The specimen itself is comprises on unremarkable tan/white\u000afibrodense tissue (50%), with the remaining tissue consisting of unremarkable\u000atan/yellow soft, lobulated adipose tissue. There are no discrete lesions,\u000agrossly. The specimen is entirely submitted, sequentially from superior to\u000ainferior.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen, sequentially submitted from superior to inferior\u000a(tissue surrounding posterior wire in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, tissue surrounding anterior wire\u000ain REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multiple frags, ESS.\u000aNote: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - superior, medial, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - superior, medial, anterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000a- superior, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - superior, medial, lateral, anterior, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000a- anterior, inferior, medial, lateral, superior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - superior, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID -\u000amedial, lateral, anterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - medial, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - medial, anterior,\u000alateral; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - medial, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - medial, lateral, anterior, posterior;\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - inferior, medial, lateral, posterior, anterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID -\u000ainferior, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - anterior, inferior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - inferior, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID -\u000ainferior, anterior, lateral, posterior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - posterior, inferior; REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID -\u000ainferior, posterior.\u000aDB/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
