VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Stefan Kraft, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Michael W Bennett, M.B., B.C.H.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFCATIONS:\u000aEXTENSIVE LOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU is present as multiple foci in REMOVED_DATE f 7\u000ablocks.\u000aAtypical ductal epithelial hyperplasia (see note).\u000aColumnar cell change with flat epithelial atypia and associated with\u000acalcifications.\u000aApocrine metaplasia.\u000aLevels examined.\u000aNOTE: There is an area of atypical ductal epithelium (0.8 cm ; slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) which\u000aconsists of monomorphic cells, predominantly flat but with focal micropapillae\u000aand cribriform spaces. This area is best classified as ADH. Selected slides\u000areviewed by Dr. S. Lester and Dr. A. Richardson who concur with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 48-year-old woman with new microcalcs on screening mammogram due to\u000asmall breast size and chest wall location, recommendation is needle\u000alocalization breast biopsy.\u000aOperation: R needle loc breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: R abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. R breast tissue.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcifications are in specimen. C.Denison, MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "R breast tissue", consists of an irregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of\u000afibroadipose tissue (3.6 cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, and\u000a1.7 cm anterior to posterior). There is a needle localization wire in place\u000aentering through the inferior lateral aspect of the specimen and exiting\u000athrough the posterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen has been previously\u000ainked per the surgeon by pathology protocol (superior red, inferior green,\u000alateral yellow, medial orange, anterior blue, and posterior black) and is\u000aserially sectioned from superior to inferior. The cut surface is variegated\u000atan/yellow to dense white epidermally involved with fibrous tissue constituting\u000aapproximately greater than 95% of the breast parenchyma. No definitive lesions\u000aor areas of disruption are grossly identified. Specimen is submitted in its\u000aentirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Superior margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections superior to inferior, block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to\u000acorrespond to adjacent breast tissue to needle localization wire, 1 frag each,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Inferior margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000asek/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Michael W Bennett M.B., B.C.H., Electronically signed on\u000a4/12/2007\u000a[report_end]
