VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christian J. Davidson, M.D.; Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST, CAUDAL, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR A PALPABLE MASS:\u000aNodule of columnar cell hyperplasia with atypia and cysts (0.8 cm) (see NOTE).\u000aNodule of sclerosing adenosis with cysts and epithelial hyperplasia (1.4 cm)\u000a(see NOTE).\u000aOther findings:\u000aSclerosing adenosis with microcalcifications.\u000aApocrine metaplasia and cysts.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: The lesion of interest was a solid mass located lateral to the nipple. On\u000ahistological examination of the entire specimen, two areas are identified which\u000amay correlate with the imaging findings; a REMOVED_DATE cm area of dense columnar cell\u000ahyperplasia (blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and a REMOVED_DATE cm area of dense sclerosing adenosis (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). Clinical and radiological correlation is needed.\u000aB. RIGHT BREAST, CRANIAL, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR A COMPLEX CYSTIC\u000aMASS AND A CLIP:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, involving an intraductal papilloma (1.9 cm) (see\u000aNOTE).\u000aBiopsy site changes are present adjacent to the intraductal papilloma.\u000aOther findings:\u000aSclerosing adenosis with focal microcalcifications.\u000aApocrine metaplasia, cysts and florid epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: The lesion of interest was a complex cystic mass located lateral to the\u000anipple, which was previously aspirated and showed reaccumulation of fluid. Per\u000aoperative report this lesion is REMOVED_DATE cm from the solid lesion excised in Part A.\u000aA REMOVED_DATE cm cystic papillary lesion is present, with florid hyperplasia and\u000aapocrine metaplasia. Several small scattered foci of architectural and\u000acytological atypia are present within the papilloma, sufficient for a diagnosis\u000aof ADH (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID). A p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID double immunostain performed on slides of\u000apapilloma highlights retained myoepithelial cells within the papilloma. Dr.\u000aAndrea Richardson has also reviewed slides of the papilloma and agrees with the\u000adiagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 56 year old woman with complex cystic mass which had aspiration and re\u000aaccumulated. Adjacent is solid suspicious lesion by ultrasound recommended for\u000aexcision.\u000aOperation: Right needle biopsy breast biopsy of two lesions.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue caudal short/superior, long/lateral.\u000aB/2. Right breast tissue cranial short/superior, long/lateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aCaudal: Mass in specimen. EP Frost, MD.\u000aCranial: Density and clip in specimen. EP Frost, MD.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname, unit number.\u000aPart A, "right breast tissue, caudal, short superior, long lateral", consists\u000aof an irregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose breast tissue (4.6\u000acm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to\u000aposterior) with a short suture designating the superior margin, and a long\u000asuture designating the lateral margin. There is a needle-localization wire in\u000aplace which enters through the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits through\u000athe medial aspect of the specimen. The specimen has been previously inked per\u000athe surgeon per pathology protocol (superior red, inferior green, medial\u000aorange, lateral yellow, anterior blue, and posterior black) and is serially\u000asectioned from medial to lateral. The cut surface is tan/yellow and homogenous\u000awith focal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately 40%\u000aof the breast parenchyma. No discrete lesions are grossly identified. The\u000aspecimen is submitted in its entirety. The specimen is received with a\u000aradiograph which demonstrates a wire and a density in the specimen.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, medial to lateral, blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to\u000ainclude fibrous area designated per specimen radiograph, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, "right breast tissue, cranial, short superior, long lateral", consists\u000aof an irregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose breast tissue (5.4\u000acm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to\u000aposterior) with a short suture designating the superior margin, and a long\u000asuture designating the lateral margin. There is a needle-localization wire in\u000aplace which enters through the inferior aspect of the specimen and exits\u000athrough the anterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen has been inked per\u000apathology protocol (superior red, inferior green, medial orange, lateral\u000ayellow, anterior blue, and posterior black) and is serially sectioned from\u000amedial to lateral. There is an irregular red/brown granular hemorrhagic\u000aill-defined area consistent with previously biopsy cavity/cystic mass (2.8 x\u000a1.8 x REMOVED_DATE cm), which comes within REMOVED_DATE cm of the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE from the\u000aposterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior\u000amargin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin.\u000aAdjacent to the previous biopsy cavity is focal area of dense white fibrous\u000atissue which contains a cervical metallic surgical clip. The remainder of the\u000abreast parenchyma is tan/yellow and homogenous with focal areas of dense white\u000afibrous issue constituting approximately 10% of the breast parenchyma. No\u000aadditional areas of disruption or discrete lesions are grossly identified. The\u000aspecimen is submitted in its entirety. The specimen is received with a\u000aradiograph which demonstrates a wire, a clip and a density in the specimen.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to include previous\u000abiopsy cavity, and area designated per specimen radiograph, block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to\u000ainclude surgical clip, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aSEK/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/6/2008\u000a[report_end]
