VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Briana Gleason, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jeffrey F Krane, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia.\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia with atypia and associated\u000amicrocalcifications.\u000aCalcifications present in association with sclerosing\u000aadenosis and columnar cell change.\u000aFibroadenoma with sclerosing adenosis (complex fibroadenoma).\u000aNOTE:\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies for E-Cadherin, p63/keratin, ER, and PR\u000aconfirm the presence of atypical lobular hyperplasia. Slides seen with Dr. D.\u000aDillon, Breast Pathology Service, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: (Needle fell out prior to procedure.)\u000aOperation: Right needle localized breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Microcalcifications - right breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. Right needle localized breast biopsy - mammo.\u000aSuture marker - long lateral, short superior.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aWire and calcs in specimen.\u000aA. Elman\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "#1 Right breast needle localized biopsy", and consists of a breast\u000aexcision specimen (5.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a localization wire in place. The\u000aspecimen has been oriented by the surgeon with two sutures. The specimen is\u000ainked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The\u000aspecimen is accompanied by a radiograph that demonstrates micro calcifications\u000ascattered throughout two-thirds of the specimen. Breast parenchyma is composed\u000aof approximately 80% fibrous tissue and 20% adipose tissue, but no discrete\u000alesions are identified. The specimen is slice from anterior to posterior and\u000asubmitted in its entirety with the exception of a small portion of adipose\u000atissue at the anterior margin and a small portion of adipose tissue at the\u000aposterior margin. All central slices are submitted, and approximately 90% of\u000athe specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin, 4 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 1, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 2, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 3, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 4, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 5, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 6, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 7, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Slice 8, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior margin, 3 frags, RSS.\u000abgl/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Briana Gleason M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jeffrey F Krane M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a5/26/2005\u000a[report_end]
