VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBROWN, FELIX, MARCEL, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BROWN,FELIX MARCEL,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "LEFT BREAST TISSUE, NEEDLE-LOCALIZATION":\u000aNodular dense hyalinized stroma.\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with benign epithelium.\u000aA fibroadenoma (0.2 cm) is present.\u000aNo tumor seen.\u000aNOTE:\u000aDense stroma may sometimes be associated with the radiographic finding of a\u000amass lesion. However, radiographic/histologic correlation is necessary to\u000aassure this biopsy represents sampling of the underlying lesion.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 44-year-old female, surgically post-menopausal, whose mammogram\u000a(8/99) - supplemented by ultrasound, demonstrated an area of stable\u000acalcifications and an area of shadowing in the left breast upper outer\u000aquadrant, for which biopsy is recommended. Patient is S/P right breast duct\u000aexcision which revealed ectasia and fibrocystic changes(4/2/99); s/P left\u000abreast core biopsy (2/18/99) of calcifications and a mass, revealing dense\u000afibrous tissue with calcifications and a fibroadenoma.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire-localized breast biopsy\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Left breast tissue\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aMass was in the specimen.\u000aJ. Meyer, MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by PHUNG,THUY LINH,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "left breast, tissue, needle-localization", and consists of five fragments\u000a(5.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate; the largest fragment measures REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm). The specimen was received unoriented, with a wire needle in place in the\u000alargest fragment; the wire was removed prior to sectioning. The external\u000asurface of the fragment was inked in black. The fragments are yellow/tan\u000afibroadipose tissue. The largest piece, with the wire needle, shows an open\u000acavity at the needle site. Cut sections reveal a white fibrous area with\u000afocal hemorrhage (1.8 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). No mass is grossly identified. The\u000aentire specimen is submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  Left breast tissue, REMOVED_DATE frag/cass, ESS\u000atlp\u000aReports to: RHEI, ESTHER M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
