VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JULIE ANN RITNER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Elizabeth M. Manion, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, CENTRAL-POSTERIOR (SITE #1), 11 G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE\u000aBIOPSIES FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aCalcifications associated with columnar cell change and microcysts.\u000aLEFT BREAST, LOWER INNER ANTERIOR (SITE #2), 11 G STEREOTACTIC CORE NEEDLE\u000aBIOPSIES FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA arising in a background of columnar cell change\u000awith associated calcifications (See Note).\u000aNOTE: Atypical ductal hyperplasia is evaluated best after excision.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHISTORY: 56 year old with BRCA-2 mutation. B mastectomy planned - 2 groups of\u000aindeterminate punctate Ca++ -- to rule out DCIS. Time in formalin: 7:55 am\u000a(site #1) and time in formalin 8:35 am (site #2),\u000aBREAST IMAGING FINDING:\u000a1. Left: 5 mm and calcifications (site #1)\u000a2. Left: 5 mm, non-palpable, and calcifications (site #2).\u000aLOCATION:\u000a1. Left: Central - posterior (site #1).\u000a2. Left: lower inner anterior (site #2).\u000aTYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY:\u000a1. 11G Stereotactic and vacuum-assisted (site #1)\u000a2. 11G Stereotactic and vacuum-assisted (site #2).\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Left breast core biopsy in two orange cassettes (site #1) - A/with Ca++\u000aand B/without Ca++s).\u000aB/2) Left breast core biopsy in two brown cassettes (site #2) - A/with Ca++ and\u000aB/without Ca++.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, in two parts, each labeled with the\u000apatient's name, unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "left breast REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", consists of two orange cassettes. The first\u000aorange cassette, labeled "A Ca++", contains multiple cylindrical fibrofatty\u000asoft tissues (1.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The second orange cassette,\u000alabeled "B", contains multiple cylindrical focally hemorrhagic fibrofatty soft\u000atissues (2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The specimen is submitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Contents of orange cassette, labeled "ACA++", multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Contents of orange cassette, labeled "B", multi frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "left breast REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", consists of two brown cassettes. The first\u000abrown cassette, labeled "A Ca++", contains multiple cylindrical fibrofatty soft\u000atissues (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm in aggregate). The second brown cassette, labeled\u000a"B", contains multiple cylindrical focally hemorrhagic fibrofatty soft tissues\u000a(2.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Contents of brown cassette, labeled "A CA++", multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Contents of brown cassette, labeled "B", multi frags, ESS.\u000ajc/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Monday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 20, 2009 at 03:57:36PM\u000a[report_end]
