VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Patricia A. R. Brunker, M.D., D. Phil.\u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. SPECIMEN LABELED "TISSUE FROM LEFT BREAST":\u000aBenign breast epithelium with a single focus of atypical ductal hyperplasia\u000a(block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), away from margin with calcifications and columnar alteration of\u000alobules with prominent snouts and secretions.\u000aCalcifications in association with benign epithelium.\u000aApocrine metaplasia and a microscopic intraductal papilloma are also\u000aidentified.\u000aB. SPECIMEN LABELED "ADDITIONAL TISSUE FROM LEFT BREAST":\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia, present as a single small focus (0.3 cm; block\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aCalcifications in association with benign epithelium.\u000aNOTE: This case has been reviewed by Dr. D. Dillon, Breast Pathology Service,\u000awho concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Calcs -> ADH, left breast\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized breast biopsy\u000aOperative Findings: None given\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Calcs -> ADH -> left breast\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Tissue from left breast\u000aB/2. Additional tissue from left breast\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aClip and wire in specimen.\u000aRadiologist: A. ELMAN\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s name and\u000aunit number.\u000aPart A, received fresh and sublabeled "tissue from left breast," consists of an\u000aunoriented discoid fragment of yellow-white soft tissue (4.1 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm),\u000awhich has a needle localization wire piercing through and through the specimen,\u000aentering approximately 1 cm from the edge of the discoid surface and exiting on\u000athe opposite surface at the disc margin. Accompanying the specimen is a\u000aradiograph depicting a soft tissue mass approximating the specimen with a\u000aneedle localization wire and a clip encircled by the radiologist. The resection\u000amargin is inked blue, and the specimen is serially sectioned revealing a poorly\u000acircumscribed, white-pink, firm area (1.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which grossly abuts\u000athe radial resection margin. Also found in this area is the localization clip,\u000ain the slice adjacent to the needle localization wire. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted for histologic evaluation.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: tissue from left breast, serially submitted, clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag\u000ain each, ESS\u000aPart B, received in 10% formalin and sublabeled "additional tissue-left\u000abreast," consists of unoriented fragment of yellow-white fibroadipose tissue\u000a(3.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), grossly consistent with breast tissue. The resection\u000amargin is inked green. The specimen is serially sectioned revealing no discrete\u000anodular abnormalities. The specimen is entirely submitted for histologic\u000aevaluation.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: additional breast tissue, serially submitted, 9 frags, ESS\u000aPAB/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Patricia A. R. Brunker M.D., D. Phil., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
