VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: MEHRA GOLSHAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Michael H Roh, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Lucian R Chirieac, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP AND MICROCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aFlorid epithelial hyperplasia with apocrine metaplasia (levels\u000aexamined) associated with microcalcifications.\u000aIntraductal papilloma with focal hyperplasia.\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with benign breast tissue.\u000aCysts including apocrine cysts.\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia.\u000aPrior biopsy site changes are present.\u000aNOTE: Dr. Dillon, Breast Pathology Service reviewed the case and\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: H/O right breast ADH, new left breast calcifications with core biopsy\u000arevealing calcifications with flattened atypia of the epithelium.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localized excisional biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast abnormal mammogram with atypical hyperplasia.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1: Left breast wire localized excisional biopsy, short-suture superior,\u000along-suture lateral to mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aClips and calcs in specimen. Kemi Bababem, MD.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "left breast wire localized excisional biopsy, short -suture\u000asuperior, long-suture lateral", and consists of REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm excision of\u000afibroadipose tissue, oriented by sutures designating superior (short-suture)\u000aand lateral (long-suture) margins. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. A localizing wire\u000aenters the anterior/inferior surface of the specimen and exits the\u000ainferior/posterior surface. The specimen is received with an accompanying\u000aradiograph demonstrating the localizing wire in place within the specimen\u000aadjacent to an area of calcification. Sectioning in the area of interest,\u000athere is a small focus of irregular-appearing dense tissue, at least REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm which exhibits dilated ducts filled with thick, yellow fluid. The\u000alesion is REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest inferior and anterior margins, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe closest superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the closest medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe closest posterior margin, and at least REMOVED_DATE cm from the nearest lateral\u000amargin. The remaining specimen is comprised predominantly of tan/yellow soft,\u000alobulated adipose tissue with no lesions. The specimen is approximately 40%\u000afibrous, 60% adipose tissue. Focal primary up to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension,\u000ain the inferior medial margin of the specimen and so at the superior/lateral.\u000aThe entire specimen is submitted sequentially from medial to lateral with area\u000aof interest in micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Entire specimen is sequentially submitted from medial to\u000alateral, multi frags, ESS.\u000aDB/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian R Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a7/21/2006\u000a[report_end]
