VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Ann K Folkins, M.D.\u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid spread.\u000aCore needle biopsy site changes.\u000aNOTE: There is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000apecimen, including margins. This case was also reviewed by Dr. S. Lester,\u000aBreast Service, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: History of abnormal right mammo, status post stereo core biopsy with\u000aDx ADH, now excisional biopsy.\u000aOperation: R needle loc BR Bx.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R mammo.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. RT BST tissue needle localized, short - superior, long - lateral to\u000amammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Mammo - Clip is seen in specimen. R. Birdwell, M.D. MJO.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh in a container, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "#1. RT BST tissue needle localization, short superior, long\u000alateral", and consists of one breast excision specimen (4.0 cm superior to\u000ainferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, and REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior) with two\u000aorienting sutures and a needle localization wire entering superiorly and\u000aexiting medially. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The medial aspect of the\u000aspecimen is disrupted. An accompanying specimen radiograph demonstrates the\u000aneedle localization wire with a clip in the center of the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is sectioned from superior to medial/inferior revealing a hemorrhagic\u000acavity extending partially along the length of the needle localization wire.\u000aNo gross mass lesion is identified. The hemorrhagic area is REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000aposterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the medial margin,\u000a1.2 cm to the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the superior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000ainferior margin. The clip is identified within the biopsy cavity and is\u000alocated REMOVED_DATE cm to the medial margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to\u000athe posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm to the anterior margin, greater than REMOVED_DATE cm to the\u000asuperior margin, and REMOVED_DATE cm to the inferior margin. The specimen is entirely\u000asubmitted from superior to inferior/medial.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Specimen submitted sequentially from superior to medial/\u000ainferior(clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags, ESS.\u000aAF/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
