VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Soft tissue biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Elizabeth A. Steensma, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, AREOLAR EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aFibroepithelial polyp.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR A MASS:\u000aFibroadenoma (0.7 cm).\u000aApocrine cysts with calcifications.\u000aNote: No carcinoma is seen after complete microscopic evaluation of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 40-year-old premenopausal women with possible density in the upper\u000aouter quadrant of the right breast. S/p right biopsy in 1999 showing\u000afibroadenoma with focal ADH. Also, has right mass in medial right areola to be\u000aexcised per the patient's request.\u000aOperation: Right breast excisional biopsy, excision right areolar mass.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram and areolar mass - right breast.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT: Mass and intact wire in the specimen. Dr. Eva Gombos.\u000aORGANS/TISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right areolar mass.\u000aB/2. Right breast tissue - long lateral, short superior, whip chest wall\u000amargin.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, "#1. Right areolar mass", consists of one unoriented fragment of\u000atan/white fibrous tissue (0.7 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm). The specimen is inked blue and\u000aentirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Areolar mass, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aPart B, "#2. Right breast tissue", consists of one excisional biopsy (3.7 x REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE cm) that is oriented by the surgeon as follows: Long stitch lateral,\u000ashort stitch superior. Specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. The specimen radiograph shows a\u000avague density at the tip of the wire and a separate denser area at the edge of\u000athe specimen.\u000aA single localization wire is noted entering the anterior aspect of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral into seven\u000aslices. There is an area of firm fibrous tissue (1.4 x REMOVED_DATE cm) along the\u000ainferior aspect of slices 3 through 5, and no other lesions are grossly\u000aidentified. The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, perpendicular, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serial sections of breast, sequential, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, perpendicular, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aEAS/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a9/15/2006\u000a[report_end]
