VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Eric L Snyder, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED RE-EXCISION FOR A PRIOR BIOPSY SITE AND\u000aCALCIFICATIONS:\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, predominantly seen adjacent to\u000athe prior biopsy site (best seen in blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID); see NOTE.\u000aCalcifications are associated with both atypical ductal hyperplasia\u000aand cysts.\u000aThe entire specimen is examined microscopically, including the margins.\u000aNOTE: The findings are similar to those seen in the prior biopsies. Atypical\u000acells line spaces with rare micropapillae or arcades. The findings are\u000ainsufficient for a diagnosis of DCIS.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. S. Lester, Breast Pathology Service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Right breast re-excision.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1 - right breast re-excision, long=lateral, short=superior, to mammo.\u000aRADIOGRAPHY REPORT: Residual calcs and wire are in specimen. A. Elman, M.D.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patients name, unit number,\u000aand "#1 - right breast re-excision, long=lateral, short=superior", and consists\u000aof an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan fibrofatty tissue (4.8 m-l x\u000a4.2 s-i x REMOVED_DATE a-p cm), which is oriented per surgeon with two sutures (short\u000asuture=superior, long suture=lateral). The specimen is inked per pro-sector\u000aaccording to the pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. There is one needle localization wire in place, which enters\u000athe lateral aspect of the specimen and exits the anteromedial aspect of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen was previously radiographed (per radiologist "residual\u000acalcs and wire are in specimen"). The specimen is serially sectioned from\u000alateral to medial to exhibit approximately 40% very dense, hemorrhagic, finely\u000acystic fibrous stroma and approximately 60% yellow/tan lobulated adipose\u000atissue. There are multiple focal areas of hemorrhage and gritty yellow/tan fat\u000anecrosis abutting the blue-inked anterior margin. The wire traverses the dense\u000afibrous tissue. No discrete masses or lesions are identified. The specimen is\u000aentirely submitted from lateral to medial as follows:\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: specimen entirely submitted from lateral to medial (dense\u000afibrous tissue traversed by wire in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID),\u000amultiple frags, ESS.\u000almb/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Eric L Snyder M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a12/21/2005\u000a[report_end]
