VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Adam Rosendorff, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZATION FOR CALCIFICATIONS AND A CLIP:\u000aLOBULAR CARCINOMA IN SITU, associated with calcifications.\u000aColumnar cell changes, fibroadenomatous changes, and epithelial\u000aHyperplasia.\u000aCalcifications associated with benign breast epithelium.\u000aNOTE: No invasive carcinoma or DCIS are seen after complete microscopic\u000aexamination of the specimen including margins.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester, Breast Pathology Service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Sent to mammo -> L breast biopsy after needle 10c long lateral, short\u000asuperior.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aClip + calcs in specimen. Dr. Yeh\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. L breast biopsy after needle loc, long lateral, short superior", and\u000aconsists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan fibroadipose tissue\u000a(4.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is oriented per surgeon with two sutures as\u000aindicated. The specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of\u000amargins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green;\u000alateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is one needle localization wire in\u000aplaced, which enters the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits the\u000aposteromedial aspect of the specimen. The specimen was previously\u000aradiographed, confirming the presence of a clip and calcifications within the\u000aspecimen (radiograph reviewed). The specimen is serially sectioned from\u000alateral to medial to exhibit approximately 50% dense/nodular white/pink fibrous\u000astroma and 50% yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue. The clip is identified\u000awithin dense fibrous stroma in the posterolateral aspect of the specimen with\u000aassociated hemorrhage and gritty yellow/tan palpable fat necrosis, coming to\u000awithin REMOVED_DATE cm of the posterior margin and REMOVED_DATE cm of the lateral margin. No\u000adiscrete masses or lesions are grossly identified. The clip and circled area\u000aof calcifications correspond to the track of the needle localization wire\u000atowards the lateral aspect of the specimen. The specimen is sequentially\u000asubmitted from lateral to medial.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin perpendicular sections, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Central specimen (clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, area of palpable calcifications in\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags, ESS.\u000aLMB/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a8/1/2007\u000a[report_end]
