VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Michael H Roh, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jonathan N Glickman, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aBreast tissue with multiple intraductal cystic papillomata\u000a(ranging in size up to REMOVED_DATE cm as measured on glass slide)\u000aand focal area of fibrosis and hemorrhage, consistent with\u000abiopsy site (see NOTE).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia (e.g. blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aImmunohistochemistry for p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID highlights myoepithelial\u000acells in the papilloma.\u000aNOTE:\u000aNo carcinoma is seen after complete microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins. The slides were reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester of the Breast\u000aPathology Service.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Abnormal core biopsy @ HVMA (path = cystic papilloma with adenosis,\u000afocal hyperplasia and focal sclerosis).\u000aOperation: Left needle-directed breast biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1 - left breast mass - short/superior, long/lateral --> fs rm/mammo.\u000aRADIOGRAPHY REPORT: Mass is seen in specimen. C. Denison, M.D. [MJD]\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient?s name, unit number,\u000aand "#1 - left breast mass, short/superior, long/lateral". The specimen\u000aconsists of a single fragment of white/yellow soft tissue, measuring REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom lateral to medial, REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior to posterior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000ainferior to superior. There is a long suture marking the lateral surface and a\u000ashort suture marking the superior surface. The specimen is inked per pathology\u000aprotocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue;\u000asuperior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. There is also a\u000awire emanating from the anterior/superior/medial aspect of the specimen.\u000aCorrelation with the radiograph suggests that the lesion is near the lateral\u000aaspect of the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned transversely along\u000athe long axis from the medial to the lateral end. Serial sectioning reveals\u000athat there is an ill-defined heterogeneous mass that is tan/brown in color but\u000acontaining dense white focal areas, first visible in slice five and ending in\u000aslice nine out of ten slices. This correlates with the tip of the wire, which\u000alies in the lesion in slice nine. The specimen is entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: medial margin (slice one), 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice two (encompassing anterior, posterior, superior, and\u000ainferior margins), 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice three, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+A: slice four, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice five, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice six, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID+REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice seven, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice eight, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: slice nine, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: lateral margin (slice ten), 5 frags, ESS.\u000aROH/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Michael H Roh M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jonathan N Glickman M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a9/22/2005\u000a[report_end]
