VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Oname O. Burlingame, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jason L Hornick, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA BORDERING ON DUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU\u000a(0.1 cm focus), associated with calcifications; see NOTE.\u000aAll surgical resection margins are negative for this focus (>0.3 cm).\u000aExtensive calcifications are also associated with benign epithelium.\u000aNOTE: This REMOVED_DATE cm focus (only present on the initial section, not on deeper\u000alevels into the block) has the morphologic appearance of low grade cribriform\u000aDCIS, but would be classified as ADH by some breast pathologists, since it is\u000aless than REMOVED_DATE cm in size. No lesional tissue remains in the block for receptor\u000astudies. Selected slides were also reviewed by Dr. Susan Lester (Breast\u000aPathologist), who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 54-year-old premenopausal woman whose REMOVED_DATE  mammogram\u000ademonstrates a 2-mm cluster of calcifications in the upper outer quadrant.\u000aOperation: L breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal L mmg.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast, short stitch=superior, long stitch=lateral with stitch on\u000achest wall margin.\u000aRADIOLOGY: The calcifications are seen at the edge of the specimen. Metal\u000ahookwire is present. Dr. Gombos.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Left breast tissue", and consists of a portion of yellow/tan lobulated\u000afibroadipose tissue (6.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) with a needle localization wire in\u000aplace (entering anterolaterally). Three orienting sutures are present (short\u000astitch equals superior; stitch equals lateral; whip stitch on chest wall\u000amargin). The specimen is inked by the surgeon per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/ orange. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned from medial to lateral, exhibiting a red/tan defect (3.0 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE \u000acm), consistent with a surgical defect. The defect communicates with and is\u000asutured at both the anterior and posterior margins. The remaining breast\u000atissue consists of approximately 90% yellow lobulated adipose tissue and 10%\u000atan/white dense fibrous stroma. As confirmed per radiology, the accompanying\u000aradiograph exhibits a needle localization wire in place and calcifications\u000awithin the specimen. No other gross lesions are identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Area of needle localization wire and probable calcifications\u000a(lateral/superior margin), multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast tissue sequentially from lateral to medial, multi\u000afrags, ESS.\u000aDFI/mmm\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jason L Hornick M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a1/19/2007\u000a[report_end]
