VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aLESTER, SUSAN, M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by LESTER,SUSAN,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED BIOPSY FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aSEVERELY ATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, involving adenosis within a\u000ahyalinized fibroadenoma in two of 10 slides (slides 3 and 4; levels\u000aexamined) (see note).\u000aCalcifications are associated with the ADH and with the fibroadenoma.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aNote: There is an area of adenosis within the fibroadenoma which is\u000aexpanded by a population of monomorphic cells with low grade nuclei.\u000aOccasional small cribriform spaces are formed. There is also pagetoid\u000aspread into the larger ducts. This cellular proliferation is limited\u000ato the fibroadenoma, and is not seen outside the area of the fibroadenoma.\u000aAlthough this proliferation may represent a small area of low grade DCIS,\u000aa diagnosis of severely atypical ductal hyperplasia is prefered due\u000ato the limited nature of the process. Dr. Andrea Richardson reviewed\u000aselected slides and concurs.\u000aThe ADH is < REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior and medial margins. Re-excision may\u000abe helpful due to the possibility of DCIS.\u000aSome of the calcifiations appear to be associated with the ADH. However,\u000athe majority of the calcifications appear to be associated with the\u000afibroadenoma.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Abnormal mammogram.\u000aOperation: Left needle directed breast bx.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: Tissue from left breast.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Ca++ in specimen. OR called. Dr. E. Frost\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by PADERA,ROBERT F.,JR.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin in one part, labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number and the description "#1 tissue from left\u000abreast". The specimen consists of a fragment of yellow/tan fibroadipose tissue\u000ameasuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The specimen is received with two orienting\u000asutures. The double long suture is present at the lateral margin and the\u000adouble short suture is present at the posterior margin. The specimen is\u000areceived six inked according to pathology protocol for the evaluation\u000aof margins: posterior = black; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior =\u000agreen; lateral = yellow; medial = orange. The specimen is received with a wire\u000ain place. The radiograph is examined and reveals the area of calcifications is\u000apresent in the inferior half of the specimen in the lateral aspect. The\u000alocalization wire is present within this area.\u000aThe tissue at the area of the calcifications is composed primarily of adipose\u000atissue with thick intervening fibrous bands and nodules. This area corresponds\u000ato a fibrous area that is primarily submitted in blocks REMOVED_DATE  The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned. The specimen is entirely submitted for histologic\u000aexamination.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  region of calcifications, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 6: representative sections of superior margin, 2 frags, RSS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of specimen, mult frags, ESS.\u000arp\u000aReports to: CHRISTIAN, ROGER, L. M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
