VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aHORNICK, JASON, L., M.D.~PH.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by HORNICK,JASON L.,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, focally severe (blocks #7 and #8)\u000a(multiple levels examined); see NOTE.\u000aNo carcinoma identified (including examination of margins).\u000aColumnar changes and hyperplasia.\u000aSclerosing adenosis.\u000aCore biopsy site changes.\u000aNOTE: This case was also reviewed by Dr. A. Richardson (Breast Pathology\u000aService), who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Right needle directed breast biopsy\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1. Right breast tissue with wire\u000aRADIOLOGY: Mass and clip in specimen. C. Denison, MD\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by SMOUSE,JASON H.,M.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number\u000aand "#1. right breast tissue with wire", and consists of a single fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (4.5 x REMOVED_DATE x 2 cm) which has been previously inked by the\u000asurgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior =\u000ablack; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow;\u000amedial = orange. The specimen is accompanied by a radiograph and report.\u000aThere is a localizing wire protruding from the lateral medial aspect of the\u000aspecimen which is removed by the pathologist. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned from superior to inferior revealing core biopsy site changes located\u000ain the inferior lateral aspect of the specimen. A single white firm\u000aill-defined area (0.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) is located adjacent to the core biopsy\u000asite changes, and abuts the lateral margin, and is greater than REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000aall other margins. The remainder of the breast parenchyma has 80% adipose\u000atissue and 20% fibrous tissue. The specimen is entirely submitted for\u000ahistologic examination. A clip is identified within the specimen and removed\u000aby the pathologist.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  breast tissue submitted from inferior to superior (clip in #4),\u000amult frags, ESS\u000ajs\u000aReports to: LESNIKOSKI, BETH-ANN M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
