VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Amend/Addenda\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast core needle biopsy\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: LISA M. ZORN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000a********** Addended Report ***********\u000aResident: Matthias D. Hofer, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER INNER, 11 G ULTRASOUND GUIDED CORE NEEDLE BIOPSIES FOR A REMOVED_DATE \u000aCM MASS:\u000aINVASIVE DUCTAL CARCINOMA, moderately differentiated (modified Bloom-Richardson\u000agrade II/III), measuring at least REMOVED_DATE cm in this limited specimen.\u000aNo lymphovascular invasion is seen in this limited specimen.\u000aDUCTAL CARCINOMA IN SITU, solid type (intermediate to high nuclear grade), is\u000apresent.\u000aNOTE: Immunohistochemistry for p63/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID confirms the absence of\u000amyoepithelium in invasive carcinoma.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHISTORY: 48 y. o. female with 5-mm enhancing mass on MRI. U/S revealed 5-mm\u000ahypoechoic round mass in left UIQ felt to conclate with MR finding.\u000aBREAST IMAGING FINDING:\u000a1. Left: 5 mm, Nonpalpable and circumscribed/lobulated mass.\u000aLOCATION:\u000a1. Left: upper inner\u000aTYPE OF CORE NEEDLE BIOPSY:\u000a1. 11G Ultrasound Vacuum-assisted\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000a1. 11G Ultrasound Vacuum-assisted time in formalin 8:55 AM - Blue cassette.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received in formalin, labeled with the patient's name, unit\u000anumber, and "8:55 AM", and consists of multiple yellow fibrofatty tissues\u000a(aggregating REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which are received in a blue cassette. The\u000aspecimens are submitted in toto.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multi frags, ESS.\u000aCP/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Thursday\u000aMay 07, 2009 at 12:15:09PM\u000aSURGICAL ADDENDUM (5/7/09):\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed on formalin fixed tissue with the\u000afollowing results for invasive carcinoma (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu (c-erb-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aImmunohistochemical studies for estrogen receptor, progesterone receptor, and\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu were evaluated by manual morphometric analysis using the following\u000acriteria:\u000aHormone receptor analysis: The percent of immunoreactive nuclei in the\u000acarcinoma is reported as follows:\u000apositive >10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000apositive - low > 0% - < 10% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000anegative 0% of nuclei immunoreactive\u000aThe lower threshold for clinically significant immunoreactivity has not\u000ayet been determined.\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu analysis:\u000aNegative (0): No immunoreactivity or immunoreactivity in <10% of tumor\u000acells.\u000aNegative (1+): Faint weak immunoreactivity in >10% of tumor cells but\u000aonly a portion of the membrane is positive.\u000aEquivocal (2+):Weak to moderate complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>10% of tumor cells or moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a< 30% of tumor cells.\u000aPositive (3+):Moderate to strong complete membrane immunoreactivity in\u000a>30% of tumor cells.\u000aCytoplasmic immunoreactivity alone is scored as a negative result.\u000aExternal controls are evaluated and show appropriate Immunoreactivity.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aAddendum #1 by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on Thursday May\u000a07, 2009 at 12:17:22PM\u000a[report_end]
