VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Elizabeth A. Steensma, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with columnar cell change and flat atypia\u000aassociated with calcifications (best seen in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aThis case was also reviewed by Dr. S. Lester, Breast Pathology Service, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Suspicious calcifications in right breast.\u000aOperation: Right breast wire localized needle biopsy.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast abnormal mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast biopsy after needle localization long suture lateral, short\u000aSuture superior to mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aCalcifications and intact hookwire in specimen. D/w with Dr. Iglehart by M.\u000aWalters, mw\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Right breast biopsy after needle localization, long suture lateral,\u000ashort superior to mammo", and consists of an irregular fragment of yellow/tan\u000ato pink/tan fibroadipose tissue (6.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which is oriented per\u000asurgeon with two sutures: Short equals superior, long equals lateral. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. There is one needle localization wire in placed entering\u000ainferolateral aspect of the specimen and exiting the posteromedial aspect of\u000athe specimen. The specimen was previously radiographed and review of the\u000aaccompanying radiograph shows the presence of calcification and an intact\u000ahookwire within the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned from lateral\u000ato medial. The cut surface is composed of approximately 40% dense/nodular\u000apink/tan focally cystic fibrous stroma (particularly in the central/medial\u000aaspect of the specimen), and approximately 60% yellow/tan adipose tissue. The\u000aneedle localization wire traverses the dense fibrous stroma in the central\u000aaspect of the specimen, corresponding to the calcifications on radiograph. No\u000adiscrete masses or lesions are grossly identified. The specimen is entirely\u000asequentially submitted from lateral to medial.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags in each,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Central specimen sequentially submitted from lateral to medial,\u000awith area of calcifications in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin perpendicularly sectioned, multi frags total, ESS.\u000alm/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a7/5/2007\u000a[report_end]
