VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aWELCH, WILLIAM, ROBERT, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by WELCH,WILLIAM ROBERT,M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "1) LEFT BREAST, BIOPSY, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR A CYST":\u000aBenign breast parenchyma with dense interlobar fibrosis, sclerosing\u000aadenosis, epithelial hyperplasia, and apocrine metaplasia.\u000aForeign material and foreign body giant cell reaction present in prior\u000abiopsy cavity.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aSPECIMEN LABELED "2) RIGHT BREAST, BIOPSY, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR A DENSITY AND\u000aA CYST":\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia (block 5).\u000aBenign breast parenchyma with nodular adenosis, sclerosing adenosis,\u000aapocrine metaplasia, columnar cell hyperplasia, and epithelial\u000ahyperplasia.\u000aForeign material and foreign body giant cell reaction present in prior\u000abiopsy cavity.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Abnormal bilateral mammogram.\u000aNo history of DCIS or carcinoma.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: #1. Left breast tissue with wire to mammo.\u000a#2. Right breast tissue with wire to mammo.\u000aRadiology Report:\u000a1) Clip in specimen.\u000aD. Smith, M.D.\u000a2) Density in specimen; clip not seen.\u000aD. Smith, M.D.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by SANTAGATA,SANDRO,M.D.,PH.D.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient's\u000aname and medical record number.\u000aPart one, labeled "#1. Left breast tissue", consists of a single breast\u000aexcision specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE cm from medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior\u000ato posterior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from superior to inferior. The specimen is inked by\u000athe surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior =\u000ablack; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow;\u000amedial = orange. There is a wire that enters the specimen at the medial\u000aportion and exits the specimen at the posterior/lateral margin. The specimen\u000ais shaped like a flat oval with the wire extending from the medial portion into\u000athe lateral portion where the tip of the wire is noted by radiology in\u000aassociation with the clip and a diffusly dense lesion. The majority of the\u000atissue in the lateral portion of the specimen is dense and fibrous and extends\u000aalong the anterior, superior, and inferior portions of the specimen. The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral and entirely submitted.\u000aThe majority of the nodule is submitted in cassettes #2 and #3. This area also\u000acontains the site of the tip of the wire (likely also to contain the\u000alocalizing clip as revealed by the radiograph) as well as foreign appearing\u000amaterial that likely represents the prior biopsy cavity.\u000aMicro 1: Medial portion of specimen, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 2: Medial portion of firm area, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 3: Lateral portion of specimen, site of wire tip and possible biopsy\u000acavity, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aPart two, labeled "#2. Right breast tissue and piece of gelocel in telpha\u000aaccompanying tissue", consists of a breast excision specimen measuring REMOVED_DATE cm\u000afrom medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from superior to inferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000aanterior to posterior. The specimen is oval in shape and is inked by the\u000asurgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior =\u000ablack; anterior = blue; superior = red; inferior = green; lateral = yellow;\u000amedial = orange. The specimen was received with the localizing wire detached\u000afrom the specimen. The specimen was radiographed by radiology and a density\u000awas seen in the specimen, although a clip was not seen. Using the radiograph\u000aas a form of orientation the wire likely entered the specimen at the\u000alateral/posterior margin and exited the specimen at the anterior/medial\u000amargin. The density is likely in the posterior/lateral portion of the\u000aspecimen. The specimen is 40% fibrous with a dense fibrous band extending\u000aalong the superior and inferior margins and along the entire posterior\u000amargin of the specimen. In the parenchyma, there is a foreign firm friable\u000amaterial that is present REMOVED_DATE cm from the posterior and lateral margins and\u000alikely represents the prior biopsy cavity. The dense fibrous tissue and the\u000aforeign material are submitted in micro REMOVED_DATE  The specimen is serially sectioned\u000afrom lateral to medial and entirely submitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  lateral margin with suspected nodule and biopsy cavity, 3 frags,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_DATE  remainder of specimen, serially toward medial margin 5 frags,\u000aESS.\u000assa\u000aReports to: LESNIKOSKI, BETH-ANN M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aADDITIONAL SPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
