VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JULIE L JONES M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Stefan Kraft, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, consisting of a solid proliferation of cells, is\u000apresent in small ducts in 3 of 5 slides (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; levels examined). The\u000aADH is associated with numerous calcifications.\u000aImmunoperoxidase studies were performed with the following results for ADH\u000a(block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID):\u000aEstrogen receptor POSITIVE\u000aProgesterone receptor POSITIVE\u000aREMOVED_ACCESSION_ID/neu NEGATIVE (1+)\u000aNote: The ADH has nuclear atypia, which raised a concern for carcinoma in\u000asitu. However, additional levels do not reveal areas sufficient for this\u000adiagnosis.\u000aThe entire specimen, including margins, was examined microscopically.\u000aSelected slides were reviewed by Dr. Andrea Richardson, who concurs with this\u000ainterpretation.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Right wire localized excisional breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast calcifications.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Rt breast wire localized excision, short stitch superior, long stitch\u000alateral.\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aWire & calcs in specimen. OR called.\u000a---SR\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Rt breast wire localized excision, short superior, long stitch\u000alateral", and consists of a portion of fibroadipose tissue (2.7 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm)\u000awith two orienting sutures ("short stitch - superior; long stitch - lateral")\u000aand a needle localization wire in place (entering at the superolateral aspect).\u000aThe specimen is inked per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from medial to lateral\u000aexhibiting a densely tan/white fibrotic parenchyma. The accompanying\u000aradiography confirms the presence of the needle localization wire and\u000acalcification within the specimen. No other gross lesions identified.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Breast tissue, multi frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000aDFI/jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a11/1/2006\u000a[report_end]
