VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aPathology Report REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000aAccessioned On: REMOVED_DATE \u000aBRODSKY, GILBERT, M.D.\u000aDIAGNOSIS: by BRODSKY,GILBERT,M.D.\u000aRIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION:\u000aBreast tissue with atypical ductal hyperplasia, columnar alteration of\u000alobules with prominent snouts and secretions, florid epithelial\u000ahyperplasia, and apocrine metaplasia.\u000aCalcifications in association with benign epithelium.\u000aFibrosis, foreign body giant cell reaction, and hemosiderin-laden\u000amacrophages consistent with core biopsy site.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the specimen,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: A 33 yo woman with R breast microcalcifications, s/p core, but\u000ano calcification seen, thought to be sampling error.\u000aOperation: R breast needle localization bx.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: R abnl mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED: R breast tissue.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcs in specimen. kb/jms\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION: by BRUNKER,PATRICIA A. R.,M.D.,D.PHIL.\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number\u000aand "right breast tissue", and consists of a rubbery, fibrofatty fragment of\u000asoft tissue (4.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which is previously inked by\u000athe surgeon per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange, and contains a needle localization wire\u000apassing from medial to lateral in the specimen, The specimen is accompanied\u000aby a radiograph showing a\u000alocalization wire passing through the\u000aspecimen and numerous (greater than 50) radiodense spots consistent with\u000amicrocalcifications. There is a small fracture in the specimen extending REMOVED_DATE \u000acm into the specimen from the posterior and superior aspects. Serial\u000asectioning from medial to lateral demonstrates multiple cystic areas, the\u000alargest of which is REMOVED_DATE cm in diameter, amongst a firm, white and tan, fibrous\u000astroma with interspersed fatty tissue. There is a white, firm, poorly\u000acircumscribed lesion (1.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) which grossly abuts the posterior\u000amargin and the superior defect in the specimen noted earlier. The specimen is\u000asubmitted medial to lateral.\u000aMicro 1: medial margin, slices 1 and 2, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro 2: slice 3, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 3: slice 4, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 4: slice 5, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 5: slice 6, demonstrating defect gap and pendulous portion, 1 frag,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro 6: slice 7, firm nodule, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 7: slice 8, more nodule, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 8: slice 9, more nodule, 1 frag, ESS.\u000aMicro 9: lateral margin, slice 10, 1 frag, RSS.\u000aMicro 10: slice 10 to demonstrate lateral margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000apab\u000aReports to: LOCKHART, DIANE, CATHERINE M.D.\u000aSPECIMEN TYPE: BREAST - EXCISIONAL TISSUE W/MARGINS\u000a[report_end]
