000aspecimen is accompanied by a radiograph, which shows microcalcifications and a\u000asurgical clip within the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned into ten\u000aslices, revealing an ill-defined, firm, fibrous area (2.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm),\u000awhich abuts the resection margin, present in slice 3 through 6. The clip is\u000apresent in slice 4. The remainder of the breast parenchyma consists of\u000aunremarkable, yellow, lobulated, adipose tissue. The entire specimen is\u000asubmitted.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Right breast tissue, with the area of the clip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID,\u000amultiple frags, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "Right breast medial wire", consists of an unoriented,\u000awire-localized breast excision specimen, measuring REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. The\u000aspecimen is received with a radiograph showing microcalcifications and a\u000alocalization wire within the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned into\u000aten slices, revealing a white, firm, fibrous, breast parenchyma with less than\u000a10% of yellow, lobulated, adipose tissue. The entire specimen is submitted for\u000amicroscopic evaluation.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Right breast, multiple frags, ESS.\u000a/MMM\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Joanna A Gibson M.D., Ph.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000a5/11/2005\u000a[report_end]
