VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DAVID I SOYBEL M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-06-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Patricia A. R. Brunker, M.D., D. Phil.\u000aPathologist: Vania Nose, MD, PhD\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST TISSUE, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A MASS:\u000aBenign breast tissue with atrophic change. See NOTE.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aNOTE: The specimen radiograph does not demonstrate the presence of the\u000aradiologic mass, which was present in the second biopsy (see below).\u000aB. RIGHT BREAST TISSUE, LABELED "DEEP MARGIN", AND INCLUDING NEEDLE TIP:\u000aIntraductal papilloma with atypical ductal hyperplasia (ADH) (0.2 cm;\u000ablock REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; levels examined; see NOTE).\u000aBenign breast tissue with atrophy and focal adenosis.\u000aThere is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aFocal fat necrosis, possibly corresponding to needle track.\u000aImmunostains for p63 and SM-MHC highlight myoepithelial cells in the\u000apapilloma.\u000aNOTE: The specimen radiograph demonstrates the radiologic mass and the\u000awire tip. The significance of ADH confined to a papilloma is unknown.\u000aThis case was reviewed by Dr. S. Lester, breast pathologist, who\u000aconcurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: Right needle loc.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. R breast tissue - mammo (main) (F.S.) - short superior, long lateral.\u000aB/2. Deep margin mammo (F.S.)\u000aRADIOLOGY REPORT:\u000aMass in specimen; path should document prior core bx site for\u000aconfirmation. (Dr. Elman)\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patients\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1 R breast tissue" consists of an REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm\u000adisrupted fragment of adipose tissue, oriented by surgeon with suture\u000adesignating superior (short suture) and lateral (long suture) margins, with a\u000aneedle-localization wire passing through the specimen. The deep surface\u000ademonstrates thin fibromembranous tissue. There is a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm loosely\u000aadherent flap of unremarkable adipose tissue at the specimen's inferior aspect.\u000aThe localization wire enters the inferior aspect of the specimen and traverses\u000amedially deep surface as fascial tissue. A radiograph bearing the patient's\u000aname and medical record number accompanies the specimen, depicting a soft\u000atissue fragment, approximating the specimen, with a wire without an arrowhead.\u000aThere is no discrete area circled by the radiologist. The specimen is\u000apartially reconstructed, and surfaces are inked per pathology protocol for the\u000aevaluation of margins: posterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red;\u000ainferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange. (Inks may not represent true\u000asurgical margin due to the markedly disrupted nature of the specimen.) The\u000aspecimen is serially sectioned revealing predominantly adipose tissue (at least\u000a95%) with occasional unremarkable tan-white fibrous tissue. The specimen is\u000aentirely submitted sequentially from superomedial.\u000aNOTE: The tissue is fragile and fragments easily upon sectioning. Loosely\u000aadherent flap of adipose tissue is removed and entirely inked black.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: entire specimen sequentially (wire tip in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), multiple frags,\u000aESS\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: loosely adherent tissue flap, multiple frags, ESS\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Deep margin more likely papilloma", is received with a\u000aradiograph demonstrating a localizing wire in place within the specimen\u000aadjacent to a small mass, encircled by the radiologist. It consists of REMOVED_DATE x\u000a4.2 x REMOVED_DATE cm fragment of unoriented adipose tissue, one surface of which is\u000acomprised of thin, fibromembranous tissue through which the localizing wire\u000atraverses. The entire specimen is inked black and sectioned, revealing a\u000acircumscribed to tan/white, firm, ovoid lesion (0.5 cm) abutting the inked\u000asurgical margin. The remaining specimen is predominantly adipose tissue (at\u000aleast 95%), with occasional tan/white fibrous tissue. There are no additional\u000alesions grossly. Focal hemorrhage is present along the localizing wire track.\u000aThe entire specimen is sequentially submitted from the localizing wire tip,\u000aentire specimen (mass in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Multiple frags, ESS\u000aDB/cdm and jp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Patricia A. R. Brunker M.D., D. Phil., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aResident Review by Patricia A. R. Brunker M.D., D. Phil., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Vania Nose MD, PhD, Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
