VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ROGER L CHRISTIAN M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Lynette M Sholl, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Lucian Chirieac, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. LEFT BREAST, WIRE-DIRECTED EXCISION, SPECIMEN #1:\u000aBenign breast tissue with focal apocrine change.\u000aFocal lactational change.\u000aFibroadenomatoid change.\u000aB. LEFT BREAST, WIRE-DIRECTED EXCISION, SPECIMEN #2:\u000aFocal epithelial hyperplasia.\u000aCalcifications in benign epithelium.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aNOTE:\u000aBoth specimens were entirely submitted for histologic examination. Slides also\u000aseen by Dr. Deborah Dillon, Breast Pathology Service, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Abnormal area of left breast; ADH vs DCIS.\u000aOperation: Wire directed excision.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: (L) breast mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Specimen one - long lateral short superior.\u000aB/2. Specimen two - long lateral short superior.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in two parts, each labeled with the patient?s\u000aname and unit number.\u000aPart A, labeled "#1. Left breast specimen #1, long lateral, short superior",\u000aconsists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan fibrofatty tissue (3.1\u000acm A-P x REMOVED_DATE cm M-L x REMOVED_DATE cm S-I), which is oriented per surgeon with two\u000asutures (short = superior, long suture = lateral) and which is inked per\u000asurgeon as follows per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. The specimen is serially sectioned from anterior to posterior\u000ato exhibit approximately 60% dense lumpy, focally cystic fibrous stroma with\u000amultiple punctate areas of hemorrhage (ranging from REMOVED_DATE cm to REMOVED_DATE cm in\u000agreatest dimension), which extends to all surgical margins, and approximately\u000a40% grossly unremarkable yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue. No discrete\u000amasses or lesions are identified. The specimen is entirely sequentially\u000asubmitted from anterior to posterior.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: (micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID = 1 bisected slice), multi frags total, ESS.\u000aPart B, labeled "#2. Left breast specimen #2, long lateral, short superior",\u000aconsists of an ovoid fragment of yellow/tan to pink/tan fibrofatty tissue (3.8\u000acm M-L x REMOVED_DATE cm A-P x REMOVED_DATE cm S-I), which is oriented per surgeon as follows:\u000ashort = superior, long suture = lateral. The specimen was previously inked per\u000asurgeon as follows per pathology protocol for the evaluation of margins:\u000aposterior/black; anterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow;\u000amedial/orange. There is one needle localization wire in place, which enters\u000athe lateral aspect of the specimen and exits the medial aspect of the specimen.\u000aPlease note that a radiograph is not received with the specimen. The specimen\u000ais serially sectioned from lateral to medial to exhibit approximately 80% very\u000adense lumpy tan/white fibrous stroma, which extends to all surgical margins and\u000aapproximately 20% unremarkable yellow/tan lobulated adipose tissue. Within the\u000adense fibrous stroma, there are multiple areas of hemorrhage (ranging from REMOVED_DATE \u000acm to REMOVED_DATE cm in greatest dimension), the largest of which may represent areas\u000aassociated with prior core biopsy procedure verses dilated hemorrhagic ducts.\u000aNo discrete masses or lesions are identified. The specimen is entirely\u000asequentially submitted from lateral to medial.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: (micro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID = 1 bisected slice), core biopsy site verses dilated\u000ahemorrhagic ducts in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, multi frags total, ESS.\u000aPlease note that the needle localization wire traverses the dense hemorrhagic\u000afibrous stroma.\u000aLmb/ajb\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Lynette M Sholl M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aPrelim Dx Release by Lynette M Sholl M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aNo Action - Prelim Sent by Lynette M Sholl M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Lucian Chirieac M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
