VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Consult slides\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JENNIFER A LIGIBEL M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-05-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kirstine Yung-Ting Oh, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Deborah A Dillon, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aCONSULT SLIDES FROM STURDY MEMORIAL HOSPITAL LAB, ATTLEBORO, MA:\u000aLEFT BREAST, EXCISION FOR A MASS WITH ADDITIONAL MARGIN (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; REMOVED_DATE ):\u000aSeverely atypical ductal hyperplasia involving an intraductal papilloma (see\u000anote).\u000aCalcifications present in association with benign epithelium.\u000aSeparately submitted "additional margin" shows columnar cell hyperplasia (see\u000anote).\u000aNOTE: By report, the main excision was received as three separate unoriented\u000atissue fragments (3.2 cm, REMOVED_DATE cm and REMOVED_DATE cm in maximum dimensions). The\u000apapillary lesion involved by severely atypical ductal hyperplasia is present in\u000aslides from all three unoriented tissue fragments, including at and <0.1 cm\u000afrom inked tissue edges. Final margins are difficult to determine. The\u000aseparately submitted ?additional margin?(by report, REMOVED_DATE cm in maximum\u000adimension) shows columnar cell hyperplasia. However, the orientation of this\u000a?additional margin? relative to the three unoriented tissue fragments\u000acontaining the papillary lesion cannot be determined. If this lesion has been\u000acompletely excised, histologic findings support the diagnosis of severely\u000aatypical ductal hyperplasia. However, the possibility of an adjacent DCIS\u000apartially involving an intraductal papilloma cannot be ruled out. Slides seen\u000aalso by Dr. Susan Lester, who concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Left breast intraductal papilloma.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left breast mass.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aConsult slides.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aReceived from Sturdy Memorial Hospital, Pathology Department, 211 Park Street,\u000aP.O. Box 2963, Attleboro, MA REMOVED_DATE  are twenty-six (26) glass slides.\u000aThe slides are labeled "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and sublabeled "AFS", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "1", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ER\u000aw/CONT", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ER NEG CONT", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID PR w/CONT", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID PR NEG CONT", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Neg CONT\u000aACTN", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID ACTIN POS CONTROL", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", "2", "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" and "REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID" through\u000a"REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID", corresponding to A) left breast excision for a mass (three segments: REMOVED_DATE \u000ax REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm, and REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) B) left breast,\u000aadditional margin, excision (3.3 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm) performed on REMOVED_DATE \u000aaccording to the accompanying pathology report bearing the patient's name.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aResident Review by Deborah A Dillon M.D., on REMOVED_DATE \u000aFinal Diagnosis by Deborah A Dillon M.D., Electronically signed on REMOVED_DATE \u000a[report_end]
