VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: DIANE C LOCKHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Leona A. Doyle, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, associated with calcifications (see NOTE).\u000aOther findings:\u000aFibroadenoma (0.4 cm)\u000aSclerosing adenosis with rare calcifications.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: The atypia sufficient for a diagnosis of ADH is best seen on slide REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aDr. Deborah Dillon has also reviewed the slides and agrees with the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 68 year old woman with clustered microcalcifications, seen on\u000ascreening mammogram left breast.\u000aOperation: Left needle localized breast biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Left abnl mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) left breast tissue with short/superior, long/lateral stitch (mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aMicrocalcifications are in the specimen.\u000aCalled to OR @ 2:19 PM, spoke with Stacy and Dr. Lockhart.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "left breast tissue, short/superior, long/lateral", and consists of an\u000airregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of fibroadipose breast tissue (4.6 cm\u000amedial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to\u000aposterior) with a short suture designating the superior margin, and a long\u000asuture designating the lateral margin. The specimen is received with a\u000aradiograph which demonstrates a wire and an area of calcifications in the\u000aspecimen. The specimen has been previously inked per the surgeon per pathology\u000aprotocol (superior/red, inferior/green, medial/orange, lateral/yellow,\u000aanterior/blue, and posterior/black), and there is a needle localization wire in\u000aplace, which enters through the lateral aspect of the specimen and exits\u000athrough the medial aspect of the specimen. The specimen is serially sectioned\u000afrom medial to lateral and the cut surface is tan/yellow and homogeneous with\u000afocal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately less than\u000a2% of the breast parenchyma. No discrete lesions or areas of disruption are\u000agrossly identified. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, medial to lateral, blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID to\u000ainclude possible area of calcification per specimen radiograph, 1 frag each,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000asek/lp\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a9/3/2008\u000a[report_end]
