VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: BRENT T SHOJI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-07-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Dick Guang-Iong Hwang, M.D., Ph.D.; Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Susan Lester, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR A CLIP AND CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia associated with columnar cell change with\u000aassociated microcalcification (best seen in blocks 18 and 21; levels examined).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia with pagetoid spread (best seen in block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aSclerosing adenosis with associated microcalcifications.\u000aFibroadenomas (up to REMOVED_DATE cm) with associated microcalcifications.\u000aCore biopsy site changes.\u000aNote: The patient had a prior core needle biopsy for calcifications showing\u000acolumnar cell change with flat atypia (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aNo malignancy is seen after microscopic examination of all fibrous tissue,\u000aincluding margins.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: None given.\u000aOperation: L needle loc.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: None given.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) L breast mammography.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Clip and calcifications in specimen. Wouden/Denison\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "#1. Left breast", and consists of a focally cauterized\u000aportion of fibrofatty tissue, with a long suture designating lateral, and a\u000ashort suture designating superior (6.5 cm from medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000asuperior to inferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm from anterior to posterior). There is an\u000ainserted needle localization wire, which inserts along the medial aspect.\u000aPlease note that the specimen is inked anterior black, posterior blue, superior\u000ared, inferior green, lateral yellow, and medial orange. The blue and black\u000ainks are different than the standard protocol. The specimen is serially\u000asectioned from medial to lateral to reveal a focally hemorrhagic cut surface\u000a(4.5 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), which extends to the anterior margin (inked black),\u000aposterior margin (inked blue), medial margin, superior margin, and inferior\u000amargin. This hemorrhagic area is REMOVED_DATE cm from the lateral margin. A surgical\u000aclip is identified within the hemorrhagic area. The remaining cut surface\u000adisplays 50% focally nodular dense white fibrous tissue, and 50% yellow\u000alobulated adipose tissue with no discrete masses or lesions identified.\u000aRepresentative sections are sequentially submitted, including all fibrous\u000aareas:\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, radially, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross sections of hemorrhagic area, 1 frag\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross sections of hemorrhagic area, with the\u000aclips identified in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross sections of the hemorrhagic area, 1\u000afrag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential bisected cross sections of hemorrhagic area, 1 frag\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections from medial to lateral, 1 frag each,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, radially, 4 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remaining fibrous areas from medial to lateral, 14 frags, ESS.\u000aCP/mmm\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Susan Lester M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a7/23/2007\u000a[report_end]
