VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Tanya Rege, M.D., Ph.D.; Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jane E. Brock, M.B., B.S., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aRIGHT BREAST WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia, focal (see NOTE).\u000aFibroadenomas (0.6 cm REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, REMOVED_DATE cm REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), both with associated calcifications.\u000aCalcifications are also present in normal breast epithelium and in sclerosing\u000aadenosis.\u000aEpithelial hyperplasia.\u000aFibroadenomatoid change.\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: An E-cadherin stain performed shows loss of membranous staining in foci\u000amorphologically suspicious for ALH, supporting the diagnosis.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 52-year-old postmenopausal female with right mammogram on REMOVED_DATE \u000ademonstrating: 1) clustered punctate calcifications in upper outer quadrant,\u000aand 2) clustered milk of calcium and punctate calcifications in upper inner\u000aquadrant.\u000aOperation: Right breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Right breast abnormal breast mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue - short stitch superior, long stitch lateral,\u000awhipstitch on chest wall margin (mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aCalcifications and wire are in the specimen.\u000aCalled to Dr. Rhei by Dr. Yeh.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "right breast tissue, short superior, long lateral, whip stitch chest wall\u000amargin", and consists of an irregular tan/yellow lobulated portion of\u000afibroadipose breast tissue (4.5 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to\u000ainferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior) with a short stitch designating the\u000asuperior margin, a long stitch designating the lateral margin, and a whip\u000astitch designating the posterior margin. There is a needle localization wire\u000awhich is loosely in place and enters through the medial aspect of the specimen.\u000aThe specimen has been previously inked per the surgeon per pathology protocol\u000a(superior red, inferior green, medial orange, lateral yellow, anterior blue,\u000aand posterior black) and is serially sectioned from medial to lateral. There\u000aare focal areas of dense white fibrous tissue constituting approximately 40% of\u000athe breast parenchyma. No discrete lesions or areas of disruption are grossly\u000aidentified. The specimen is submitted in its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections medial to lateral, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000asek/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jane E. Brock M.B., B.S., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a7/3/2008\u000a[report_end]
