VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Paul A. VanderLaan, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: William R Welch, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia, focal (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID).\u000aColumnar cell hyperplasia with microcalcifications,\u000acysts, and apocrine change.\u000aBiopsy site changes.\u000aThe margins were inked and evaluated for the final\u000adiagnosis.\u000aNOTE: Dr. J. Brock reviewed the case and concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 48 year old nulliparous premenopausal female whose REMOVED_DATE mammograms\u000ademonstrates right breast calcifications. Diagnostic mammogram on REMOVED_DATE \u000areveal clustered LOQ calcifications and UOQ assymetry. Core biopsy of ca++ and\u000au/s of the asymmetry recommend core biopsy REMOVED_DATE  revealed ADH, u/s was\u000aunrevealing. Excision recommended.\u000aOperation: Right breast wire localized biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: None given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal R mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Right breast tissue short suture superior, long lateral with stitch on\u000achest wall margin - mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Calcification and into it wire are within the specimen. No clip is\u000aseen. Dr. Gombos.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, medical record\u000anumber, and "#1. Right breast tissue short suture superior, long lateral, whip\u000astitch on chest wall margin", consists of a REMOVED_DATE gram lumpectomy that was\u000aoriented per the surgeon such that a short suture designates superior, a long\u000asuture designates lateral, and a stitch designates the chest wall margin. The\u000aspecimen measures REMOVED_DATE cm superior to inferior, REMOVED_DATE cm medial to lateral, and\u000a2.5 cm anterior to posterior. The specimen is received inked by the surgeon in\u000athe OR per pathology protocol, with red superior, green inferior, black\u000aposterior, blue anterior, orange medial, and yellow lateral. Accompanying the\u000aspecimen is a radiograph illustrating a wire entering the anterior aspect of\u000athe specimen, but not exiting the specimen. This wire is localizing a presumed\u000aprevious biopsy cavity, but no clip is identified on the radiograph. There is\u000aalso an area of calcifications that were not targeted. The specimen is fixed,\u000aserially sectioned from posterior to anterior, to reveal approximately a 50%\u000afirm white fibrous tissue and 50% yellow lobulated fatty tissue composition.\u000aNo clip is noted in the specimen. The entire specimen is submitted for\u000ahistology.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Posterior margin, perpendicular sections, 5 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Serially sections from posterior to anterior, 1 frag per cassette,\u000aESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Anterior margin, perpendicular sections, 3 frags per cassette,\u000aESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID\u000apva/ag\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by William R Welch M.D., Electronically signed on Monday\u000aREMOVED_MONTH 06, 2009 at 05:08:56PM\u000a[report_end]
