VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: ESTHER RHEI M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-08-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Astrid Weins, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPathologist: Jeffrey F Krane, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, UPPER OUTER, WIRE LOCALIZED EXCISION FOR CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aATYPICAL DUCTAL HYPERPLASIA, associated with columnar cell change and\u000acalcifications (block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID; levels examined).\u000aApocrine cysts.\u000aNote: There is no malignancy seen upon microscopic examination of the\u000aspecimen, including margins.\u000aThe slides were reviewed by Dr. S. Lester, Breast Pathology Service,\u000awho concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: 33 year old female with a family history of breast cancer and left\u000abreast ultrasound on REMOVED_DATE  revealing a cluster of punctate and indistinct\u000acalcifications in upper outer quadrant - not amenable to core biopsy. Also,\u000astatus post right biopsy on REMOVED_DATE  for palpable mass revealing benign tissue.\u000aOperation: Left breast wire localization biopsy.\u000aOperative Findings: Not given.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal left mammogram.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1. Left breast tissue - stitches: long - lateral, short - superior,\u000awhipstitch - deep. To FS room for mammo.\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aCalled to OR. Calcifications in specimen.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number\u000aand "left breast tissue", and consists of a tan/yellow lobulated portion of\u000afibroadipose breast tissue (2.8 cm medial to lateral, REMOVED_DATE cm superior to\u000ainferior, and REMOVED_DATE cm anterior to posterior) with a short suture designating the\u000asuperior margin, a long suture designating the lateral margin, and a whipstitch\u000adesignating the deep/posterior margin. The specimen radiograph reveals a faint\u000acluster of calcifications near the wire. There is a needle-localization wire\u000ain place which enters through the anterior inferior aspect of the specimen\u000aconnecting through the posterior aspect of the specimen. The specimen has been\u000apreviously inked per pathology protocol per the surgeon (superior red, inferior\u000agreen, medial orange, lateral yellow, anterior blue, and posterior black) and\u000ais serially sectioned from medial to lateral. The cut surface is dense white\u000aand fibrous and counts for approximately greater than 90% of the breast\u000aparenchyma. No discrete lesions are grossly identified. The specimen is\u000asubmitted in its entirety. The blocks are radiographed and calcifications are\u000aidentified in blocks REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID and REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections to include possible areas of\u000acalcification adjacent to needle-localization wire per specimen radiograph\u000a(block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID), 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, 2 frags, ESS.\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_CASE_ID.\u000asek/elw\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jeffrey F Krane M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed on\u000a9/10/2008\u000a[report_end]
