VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: KATHARINE CARTER M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Christian J. Davidson, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Jacqueline M William, M.D., Ph.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aLEFT BREAST, WIRE-LOCALIZED EXCISIONAL BIOPSY:\u000aAtypical ductal hyperplasia with associated microcalcifications. (multiple\u000afoci).\u000aAtypical lobular hyperplasia\u000aCalcifications associated with columnar cell change and adenosis.\u000aPapilloma\u000aImmunohistochemical studies performed at BWH confirm the absence of E-Cadherin\u000aexpression in the areas of ALH.\u000aThe immunoperoxidase, immunofluorescence and in-situ hybridization tests\u000aperformed at Brigham and Women's Hospital were developed and their performance\u000acharacteristics determined by the Immunohistochemistry Laboratories in the\u000aDepartment of Pathology at BWH. They have not been cleared or approved by the\u000aU.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). The FDA has determined that such\u000aclearance or approval is not necessary.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Calcs LUOQ not amenable to core.\u000aOperation: Left mammography directed breast bx.\u000aOperative Findings: Not provided.\u000aClinical Diagnosis: Abnormal mammogram left breast.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) left breast tissue - left upper/outer quadrant - short/superior,\u000along/lateral (mammo).\u000aRADIOLOGY:\u000aTarget - amorphous Ca++ - called to OR - good specimen.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, in one part, labeled with the patient's name,\u000aunit number, and "L breast tissue", and consists of an irregular fragment of\u000afibroadipose tissue (7.5 cm S-I x REMOVED_DATE cm M-L x REMOVED_DATE cm A-P) with a short suture\u000adesignating superior and a long suture designating lateral per surgeon. The\u000aspecimen is inked per pathology. The specimen is inked by the surgeon per\u000apathology protocol for the evaluation of margins: posterior/black;\u000aanterior/blue; superior/red; inferior/green; lateral/yellow; medial/orange.\u000aThe specimen is received with a radiograph and a needle localization wire\u000aentering through the lateral aspect with no definitive exit. The specimen is\u000aserially sectioned from superior to inferior to exhibit an area of hemorrhage,\u000aconsistent with needle localization wire placement. Surrounding the area of\u000ahemorrhage are foci of gritty/granular fibrous areas. No definitive masses or\u000alesions can be grossly appreciated. The breast parenchyma consists of 75%\u000adense white fibrous tissue to 25% yellow lobulated adipose tissue. The entire\u000aspecimen is submitted for routine processing.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular superior margin, 2 frags in each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Body, sequentially submitted from superior to inferior, 1 frag in\u000aeach, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Perpendicular inferior margin, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Remainder of specimen, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000ajc/lp\u000aCASE NUMBER: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID.\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Jacqueline M William M.D., Ph.D., Electronically signed\u000aon Tuesday REMOVED_MONTH 07, 2009 at 03:23:49PM\u000a[report_end]
