VAccession Number: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID Report Status: Final\u000aType: Surgical Pathology\u000aSpecimen Type: Breast, excisional tissue\u000aProcedure Date: REMOVED_DATE \u000aOrdering Provider: JAMES D IGLEHART M.D.\u000aCASE: BS-09-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID\u000aPATIENT: REMOVED_PATIENT_NAME\u000aResident: Kyle L. Viani, M.D.\u000aPathologist: Alessandra F Nascimento, M.D.\u000aPATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:\u000aA. RIGHT BREAST TISSUE, WIRE LOCALIZATION FOR A CLIP AND CALCIFICATIONS:\u000aMicrocalcifications associated with apocrine cysts, epithelial\u000ahyperplasia, sclerosing adenosis and intraductal papilloma.\u000aColumnar cell change.\u000aThe margins are inked and evaluated for final diagnosis.\u000aNOTE: This case was also reviewed by Dr. Jane Brock, Breast Pathology Service,\u000awho concurs.\u000aCLINICAL DATA:\u000aHistory: Rt breast atypical ductal hyperplasia.\u000aOperation: Rt breast biopsy needle local.\u000aTISSUE SUBMITTED:\u000aA/1) Right breast needle local biopsy; suture: Long -lateral, short -\u000asuperior.\u000aRADIOLOGY: Specimen radiographs demonstrate the intact wire and calcifications\u000aboth anterior and posterior to the wire; the clip is also included.\u000aGROSS DESCRIPTION:\u000aThe specimen is received fresh, labeled with the patient's name, unit number,\u000aand "#1. Right breast needle local biopsy, suture at long lateral, short\u000asuperior", consists of an irregular portion of yellow lobulated fibrofatty\u000atissue (3.2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm), with a long suture designating lateral, and a\u000ashort suture designating superior. There is an inserted needle localization\u000awire, displayed on the accompanying radiograph, which enters along the\u000aanterior-superior margin, and superficially exits at the inferior-lateral\u000amargin. The specimen is inked by the prosector, per pathology protocol as\u000aanterior blue, posterior black, superior red, inferior green, lateral yellow,\u000aand medial orange. The specimen is serially sectioned to reveal a surgical\u000aclip, as also displayed on the accompanying radiograph. The clip is less than\u000a0.1 cm from the lateral margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the anterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from\u000athe posterior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the superior margin, REMOVED_DATE cm from the inferior\u000amargin, and REMOVED_DATE cm from the medial margin. The remaining cut surface displays\u000a70% yellow lobulated adipose tissue, and 30% white fibrous tissue, with no\u000adiscrete masses or lesions identified. The specimen is sequentially submitted\u000ain its entirety.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Lateral margin, radially, multi frags, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, with the surgical clip\u000aidentified in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, with the possible area of\u000aconcern on the accompanying radiograph in REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, containing the possible area\u000aof concern on the accompanying radiograph, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, containing the possible area\u000aof concern on the accompanying radiograph, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross section, bisected, containing the possible area\u000aof concern on the accompanying radiograph, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, bisected, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Sequential cross sections, 1 frag each, ESS.\u000aMicro REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID: Medial margin, radially, 3 frags, ESS.\u000aCP/mmm\u000aBy his/her signature below, the senior physician certifies that he/she\u000apersonally conducted a microscopic examination ("gross only" exam if so stated)\u000aof the described specimen(s) and rendered or confirmed the diagnosis(es)\u000arelated thereto.\u000aFinal Diagnosis by Alessandra F Nascimento M.D., Electronically signed on\u000aWednesday August 26, 2009 at 09:51:58AM\u000a[report_end]
