. Left breast, needle core biopsy at 12 o'clock: Fibroadenoma with rare microcalcifications. Extremely minute focus of atypical ductal hyperplasia bordering on low grade micropapillary ductal carcinoma in situ; see note. Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1125 PAT.TOCC REMOVED_DATE  - 1136 MD.MIHN DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT: NOTE: In specimen B, in the breast tissue adjacent to the fibroadenoma, there is a very small focus of atypical cells that is at least atypical ductal hyperplasia. This focus resembles the ductal carcinoma in situ seen in specimen A. However, given the very small size of this focus, a definitive diagnosis of ductal carcinoma in situ cannot be made. An immunohistochemical stain for estrogen receptors will be performed on specimen A and the results will be reported in an addendum to follow. Final Diagnosis by: Nicole A. Mihok, M.D. Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1127 PAT.TOCC REMOVED_DATE  - 1129 PAT.TOCC REMOVED_DATE  - 1139 MD.MIHN Notes: REMOVED_DATE  In addition to the co-signer, Drs. Misialek, Ross, Feen and Mattia have seen the case and concur with the above written diagnoses. Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1128 PAT.TOCC ADDENDUM REPORTAddendum #1 Entered: REMOVED_DATE -1633 *** ADDITIONAL DIAGNOSTIC INFORMATION *** This report contains an addendum to the original for the purpose of providing additional diagnostic information. The original diagnosis is unchanged. Immunohistochemical staining on the formalin-fixed, paraffin-embedded block REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID reveals these results for the ductal carcinoma in situ: Estrogen receptor: POSITIVE (>10% of nuclei stained) Estrogen receptor (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) and/or progesterone receptor (PgR 1REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) is assessed visually by quantitation of the percent of tumor cells staining.Addendum Final: MIHOK,NICOLE MD***** * REMOVED_DATE  <10/29/08-1639> by MD.MIHN Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1633 PAT.DORDCODES: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Positive REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Obstruction, NO REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Tumor cells