VREMOVED_CASE_ID SHILAS,DALIA 41/F <REG REF REMOVED_DATE > (REMOVED_CASE_ID) WIC GRABOWSKY,TARA A. M.D.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Specimen: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID:REMOVED_CASE_ID SOUT Received: REMOVED_DATE -1148 Spec Type: - Clinical diagnosis & history: Baseline mammogram showed multiple clusters of indeterminate calcifications of right retroareolar lower central and upper outer quadrant; ? fibrocystic changes vs ductal carcinoma in situ Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1150 PAT.MURJ Gross description: The specimen is received in formalin in one container, labeled "stereo biopsies, right breast calcifications" and consists of two aqua cassettes, which are designated on the specimen requisition as the right retroareolar lower central breast with calcifications. The first cassette contains three firm, yellowish-red needle biopsies ranging in size from REMOVED_DATE cm. in length x REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter to REMOVED_DATE cm. in length x REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter. 3X all, labeled 1. The second aqua cassette contains a 2 x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. aggregate of reddish-tan, firm, glistening needle biopsies, each measuring REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter. 1 agg. all, labeled 2. Also present in the same container is a 2 x 1 x REMOVED_DATE cm. aggregate of reddish-tan, firm needle biopsies with attached blood clot, each measuring REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter. 1 agg. all, labeled 3. Dictated by: LV Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1202 PAT.MURJ DIAGNOSIS: Right breast, lower central retroareolar, needle biopsy: Calcifications associated with atypical ductal hyperplasia. Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1028 PAT.MURJ DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT: NOTE: This case was reviewed by Dr. Frederick Koerner, Massachusetts General Hospital, who rendered the following opinion: "I find this case especially challenging and I cannot give you a precise diagnosis. The calcifications of concern distort several ducts and ductules, and the associated ductal cells look atypical. They appear enlarged and they possess large and somewhat pleomorphic nuclei and homogeneous chromatin. A few nuclei contain conspicuous nucleoli. I would not interpret these areas as foci of ductal hyperplasia; the nuclei appear too big, and the presence of calcifications would not fit with that interpretation. The cells could represent a carcinoma; however, the foci do not exhibit an obvious increase in the number of epithelial cells. In the absence of cellular proliferation and marked anaplasia, I cannot recognize the cells as malignant. Degenerative changes can sometimes cause nuclear enlargement and pleomorphism, and such a phenomenon might explain the appearance of these cells. These degenerative effects usually cause the chromatin to become dark and smudgy rather than pale, as seen in the case at hand, so I do not feel comfortable with this interpretation, DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT: (Continued) either. I would therefore make the diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia and suggest that the patient undergo an excision of this region of the right breast. After examining the adjacent tissue, I expect that you will be able to reach a secure diagnosis." Final Diagnosis by: Jean L. Fraser, M.D. Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1033 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1107 MD.FRAJE Notes: REMOVED_DATE  All nine slides ret'd /jam (CR) REMOVED_DATE  Path report faxed /jam REMOVED_DATE  Dr. Koerner's report rec'd /djd REMOVED_DATE  Sent all nine slides to Dr. Frederick Koerner @ MGH via courier for opinion JLF/jam (CO) (617) REMOVED_DATE Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 0846 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1022 PAT.DORD REMOVED_DATE  - 1123 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1137 PAT.MURJCODES: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - Right breast, N BREANBX - -BREANBX BREANBX-REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID - -BREANBX-Atypical hyperp Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1150 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1028 PAT.MURJCOPIES TO: GRABOWSKY,TARA A. M.D. P.O. Box 469 MEDFIELD, MA REMOVED_CASE_ID (508)359-8141 SELLAND,DONNA-LEE M