VREMOVED_CASE_ID PAYNTER,MARTHA (P) 49/F <REG REF REMOVED_DATE > (REMOVED_CASE_ID) WIC FINN,LEONARD M. M.D.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------Specimen: REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID:REMOVED_CASE_ID SOUT Received: REMOVED_DATE -1324 Spec Type: - Clinical diagnosis & history: 5 mm. cluster of indeterminate calcifications, left posterior upper outer quadrant; ? fibrocystic changes vs ductal carcinoma in situ Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1325 PAT.MURJ Gross description: The specimen is received in formalin in one container, labeled "stereotactic loc reduced SVC, left breast calcifications" and consists of two cassettes. The first, which is purple in color and designated on the specimen requisition to be the upper outer quadrant with calcifications, consists of three firm, yellow, glistening needle biopsies measuring 2 cm. in length x REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter to 3 cm. in length x REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter. 3X all, labeled 1. The second, which is yellow in color and designated on the specimen requisition to be the remainder of the tissue, consists of a REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE x REMOVED_DATE cm. aggregate of yellowish-red, firm needle biopsies measuring REMOVED_DATE cm. in diameter each. 2 agg. all, labeled REMOVED_DATE  Dictated by: LV Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1338 PAT.MURJ DIAGNOSIS: Left breast, posterior upper outer quadrant, needle core biopsies: Microcalcifications identified and associated with atypical ductal hyperplasia, columnar type (see note). Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1025 PAT.DORD REMOVED_DATE  - 1150 PAT.MURJ DESCRIPTIVE COMMENT: NOTE: This case was sent to dr. Fritz Koerner at Massachusetts General Hospital. He concurs and adds "The tissue in the background of this core biopsy specimen shows stromal atrophy. You have marked a small duct and several terminal duct-lobular units distended by a population of atypical ductal cells. They appear enlarged and they possess slightly enlarged, smoothly contoured, oval or round nuclei; homogeneous chromatin; inconspicuous nucleoli; and modest amounts of eosinophilic, apical cytoplasm. Most of the cells grow in a single, sometimes crowded or pseudostratified layer; however, those in a few foci seem to show genuine stratification and the formation of miniature micropapillary tufts. The presence of these cytological alterations identifies the cells as atypical, and the crowding and stratification of the cells imply a significant degree of proliferation of the atypical population. Because of these findings, I would not hesitate to make the diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia of columnar cell type. I share your concerns about the frequent lack of significant findings in specimens excised because of a diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia. Approximately sixty percent of our cases showing atypical columnar cell hyperplasia on a core biopsy specimen fall into this category. Perhaps we will learn criteria that will allow us to reduce this percentage, but I would not back away from the diagnosis of atypical ductal hyperplasia in the case at hand." Final Diagnosis by: Nicole A. Mihok, M.D. Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1157 PAT.MURJ Notes: REMOVED_DATE  Received all 9 slides and report from Dr Winer via mail. NAM/td REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID REMOVED_DATE  Sent all nine slides to Dr. Eric Winer @ DFCI via courier at the req of Leanna/NWH Breast Center /jam (REMOVED_ACCESSION_ID) (617) REMOVED_DATE (Dr Winer) REMOVED_DATE  Rec'd all nine slides from Dr. Koerner /dg CR REMOVED_DATE  Second report faxed from MGH /jam REMOVED_DATE  Rec'd path report from Dr. Koerner via fax /dg REMOVED_DATE  Sent nine slides (1 1L-3L, REMOVED_DATE L-3L and REMOVED_DATE L-3L) to Dr. Frederick Koerner @ MGH via courier for opinion NAM/jam (CO) (617) REMOVED_DATE Edited by: REMOVED_DATE  - 1156 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1125 PAT.GILD REMOVED_DATE  - 1251 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1403 PAT.GILD REMOVED_DATE  - 0731 PAT.MURJ REMOVED_DATE  - 1339 PAT.CORCCODES: